var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#KDK","page":"KDK","text":"The KDK module implements the split-step Fourier method using a \"kick–drift–kick\" (KDK) algorithm to evolve the Schrödinger–Poisson equation. The scheme is a second-order Strang splitting that decouples the kinetic and potential parts of the Hamiltonian.\n\nThe full mathematical derivation is given in Algorithms §2; this page documents the two low-level entry points that the main loop calls.","title":"KDK"},{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#Core-Functions","page":"KDK","text":"","title":"Core Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#apply_kick_step!","page":"KDK","text":"Each call performs step (c), then step (a) of Algorithm 1 in the paper: it computes the gravitational potential from the current wavefunction (including the tidal field, if enabled), applies the half-timestep potential operator, and returns the Fourier transform of the kicked wavefunction ready for the drift step. The closing half kick, step (d), is applied by the next iteration's kick call; with KDK_flag = true the kick length is dt/2, so consecutive kick–drift–kick steps form the symmetric Strang sequence.\n\nThe implementation handles:\n\ndevice_ψ is consumed by this function on the GPU path (the underlying buffer is freed before returning). The caller must not reuse the binding.\nOn the GPU, intermediate buffers (device_Φ_all, device_Φ_WaveDM, potential_grav_static, device_V, potential_all, nonlinear_term, device_ψ) are released through CUDA.unsafe_free! so peak GPU memory stays close to a single wavefunction's worth.\nThe tidal field (if MW_tidal_field = true) is added to Phi_rm total at this point.","title":"apply_kick_step!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#apply_drift_step!","page":"KDK","text":"The drift step fuses step (b) of Algorithm 1 with the absorbing boundary: it multiplies the Fourier-space wavefunction by the linear phase factor e^inabla^2Delta t2, transforms back to real space, and applies the absorbing boundary mask mathcal B(mathbf r).\n\nThe spec and linear_phase arguments are consumed by this function; their bindings should not be reused after the call.","title":"apply_drift_step!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#Algorithm-Description","page":"KDK","text":"Write the SPE evolution operator as the sum of two pieces:\n\npartial_t psi = bigl(hat D + hat Nbigr)psi\n\nwith the kinetic dispersion operator\n\nhat D = tfraci2nabla^2\n\nand the potential operator\n\nhat N = -iPhi_rm total\n\nThe second-order Strang split applied over a single timestep Delta t is\n\npsi(mathbf r t+Delta t) approx e^fracDelta t2hat Ne^Delta t hat De^fracDelta t2hat Npsi(mathbf r t)\n\nIn Fourier space the drift is diagonal: e^Delta t hat D to e^-fraci2Delta tk^2.","title":"Algorithm Description"},{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#Steps-(c)-(a)-—-apply_kick_step!","page":"KDK","text":"Compute the gravitational potential from the current density:\nrho = psi^2 qquad\nPhi_rm DM(mathbf k) = -frac4pik^2biglrho(mathbf k) - langlerhorangledelta(mathbf k)bigr\nIf MW_tidal_field = true, add the time-dependent MW (+LMC) tidal field to Phi_rm total.\nEvaluate the user-supplied potential V(mathbf r t psi) on the grid and assemble Phi_rm total.\nApply the half-timestep potential kick to the wavefunction:\npsi leftarrow e^fracDelta t2hat Npsi = e^-iPhi_rm totalDelta t2psi\nTransform to Fourier space and return the spectrum for the drift step.","title":"Steps (c) + (a) — apply_kick_step!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#Step-(b)-—-apply_drift_step!","page":"KDK","text":"Multiply the Fourier-space wavefunction by the linear phase:\ntildepsi leftarrow e^inabla^2Delta t2tildepsi = e^-ik^2Delta t2tildepsi\nInverse Fourier transform back to real space: psi leftarrow mathcal F^-1tildepsi.\nApply the absorbing boundary mask mathcal B(mathbf r):\npsi leftarrow psi odot mathcal B(mathbf r)\n\nThe boundary mask is set up by setup_absorption_boundary(Xmax, Ymax, Zmax, x, y, z, absorb_coeff, dt). It multiplies psi by\n\nmathcal B(mathbf r)\n= expleft-alphaleft(6-sum_textfacestanhfracx_ipm L2L50right)Delta tright\n\nso the damping is strongest at the box faces and decays smoothly into the interior over a transition layer of width sim L50. The factor is not strictly zero at the boundary; with the default alpha=10 it is exp(-alphaDelta t) at a face for a field that is uniform in the other two directions.","title":"Step (b) — apply_drift_step!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#Sign-Convention","page":"KDK","text":"Operator Definition Evolution factor\nKinetic hat D tfraci2nabla^2 e^Delta t hat D to e^-fraci2Delta t k^2\nPotential hat N -iPhi_rm total e^fracDelta t2hat N = e^-iPhi_rm totalDelta t2\nDrift in code exp.(im .* Laplacian .* dt/2) matches e^-i k^2 Delta t2 for the convention k to (2pi k_rm code)\nKick in code exp.(-im .* potential_all .* dt_kick) matches e^-iPhiDelta t2\n\nThe drift implementation in setup_fft_operators uses the convention\n\nnabla^2 longrightarrow -(2pi k_x)^2 - (2pi k_y)^2 - (2pi k_z)^2\n\nso linear_phase = fftshift(exp.(im * Laplacian * dt/2)) evaluates to the same diagonal factor as the mathematical form above.","title":"Sign Convention"},{"category":"section","location":"api/KDK/#Memory-Management","page":"KDK","text":"Path Strategy\nGPU (gpu = true) All device buffers created in apply_kick_step! and apply_drift_step! are released through CUDA.unsafe_free! as soon as they are no longer needed. Peak GPU memory stays close to the size of one psi array.\nCPU Julia's GC reclaims host arrays when bindings go out of scope; no manual release is needed.\n\nThe internal helper _release!(buf, gpu) wraps the conditional in a single function so that call sites remain readable.","title":"Memory Management"},{"category":"function","location":"api/KDK/#WaveDM.apply_kick_step!","page":"KDK","text":"apply_kick_step!(\n    device_ψ,\n    ψ,\n    V::Function,\n    rho,\n    rho_max,\n    rho_max_id::CartesianIndex{3},\n    grid::SimulationGrid,\n    config_gravity::GravityConfig,\n    config_tidal::TidalFieldConfig,\n    config_device::DeviceConfig,\n    dt::Real,\n    i::Int64,\n    t::Vector{<:Real}\n) -> Tuple{Any, Any, Any}\n\n\nApply a full kick step including gravitational potential computation. This function encapsulates the entire kick step from density computation to potential application.\n\nArguments\n\ndevice_ψ: Device-resident wavefunction.  Consumed by this function: the underlying GPU buffer (if any) is freed before the function returns and the Julia binding on the caller side is invalidated.\nψ: Host-resident wavefunction (used for evaluating the external potential V).\nV: Potential function with signature V(x, y, z, ψ) -> scalar.\nrho_max: Maximum density value (kept for backward compatibility; not used).\nrho_max_id: Cartesian index of maximum density (used as the centering point for the tidal potential correction).\ngrid: SimulationGrid (contains: xxx, yyy, zzz, Δ, Nx, Ny, Nz, unitcellvolumn, oneMatrix).\nconfig_gravity: GravityConfig (constants only).\nconfig_tidal: TidalFieldConfig (constants only).\nconfig_device: DeviceConfig (constants only: gpu, DeviceArray).\ndt: Time step (variable, passed as parameter).\ni: Current iteration index (variable, passed as parameter).\nt: Time vector (variable, passed as parameter).\n\nReturns\n\nΦ_all: Host-resident total gravitational potential.\nΦ_WaveDM: Host-resident WaveDM-only gravitational potential.\nspec: Device-resident Fourier transform of the kicked wavefunction, ready for the drift step.\n\nMemory management\n\nOn the GPU path, the temporary device buffers that this function creates (device_Φ_all, device_Φ_WaveDM, potential_grav_static, device_V, potential_all, nonlinear_term, and device_ψ) are released through CUDA.unsafe_free! as soon as they are no longer needed so that peak GPU memory stays close to the size of one wavefunction array.  On the CPU path Julia's GC will reclaim the corresponding host arrays when their bindings go out of scope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.apply_kick_step!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/KDK/#WaveDM.apply_drift_step!","page":"KDK","text":"apply_drift_step!(\n    spec,\n    linear_phase,\n    border,\n    gpu::Bool,\n    DeviceArray\n) -> Any\n\n\nApply a full drift step including boundary condition. This function encapsulates the entire drift step from Fourier space transformation to boundary application.\n\nspec and linear_phase are consumed by this function; their bindings on the caller side should not be reused after the call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.apply_drift_step!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#Configs","page":"Configs","text":"WaveDM.jl groups its simulation parameters into a small set of immutable configuration structures. The structures are not strictly required — every field is also exposed as a keyword argument to simulate_waveDM / SPE3D_waveDM — but they make it easy to thread a complete configuration through helper functions without a long argument list.\n\nAll fourteen structures below are exported from the WaveDM module.","title":"Configs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#1.-Common-structures","page":"Configs","text":"","title":"1. Common structures"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#SimulationGrid","page":"Configs","text":"Carries the grid geometry and the precomputed coordinate arrays that the rest of the code uses. Fields: Xmax, Ymax, Zmax, Nx, Ny, Nz, Δ, x, y, z, xxx, yyy, zzz, r, oneMatrix, unit_cell_volumn.","title":"SimulationGrid"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#DeviceConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Thin compatibility wrapper around PhysicalMeshes.ParallelBackend. The preferred way to construct a backend is now select_backend, which auto-detects the best kind (:serial / :threads / :distributed / :gpu) for the current process.\n\nbackend::ParallelBackend — the actual backend object.\nDeviceArray — convenience alias of backend.device_array (the conversion function: cu on GPU, collect on CPU).\nDA — convenience alias of backend.distribute_array (the array constructor: DArray for distributed runs, collect otherwise).\ngpu::Bool — true dispatches to CUDA.jl, false stays on CPU.\nkind::Symbol — one of :serial, :threads, :distributed, :gpu.\nnthreads, nworkers, pids — process topology detected by detect_resources.\n\nConstructors:\n\nDeviceConfig(; gpu=false, distributed=false, kind=:auto, ...) — full auto-detection, accepting the same kwargs as select_backend.\nDeviceConfig(gpu::Bool, device_array, distribute_array) — backward-compat positional constructor that builds a minimal backend without auto-detection.\n\nAll call sites of the old .gpu / .DeviceArray / .DA fields keep working through property forwarding.","title":"DeviceConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#TimeStepConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Holds the time vector and step. Fields: t::Vector{T}, dt::T, Nt::Int, KDK_flag::Bool.","title":"TimeStepConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#AstroUnitsConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Holds the conversion factors between dimensionless simulation units and physical astrophysical units. Fields: length_astro, time_astro, mass_astro, density_astro, acc_astro, velocity_astro, potential_astro, uT, uL, uVel, uAcc, uRho, uMomentum, h_astro, aₛ_astro, mₐ_astro, c_astro, κ_astro, G0, a0.","title":"AstroUnitsConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#2.-Kick-step-structures","page":"Configs","text":"","title":"2. Kick-step structures"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#GravityConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Passed to apply_kick_step! each iteration. Fields: boundary, Φ_b, sim_mesh_force, mesh_particles, SofteningLength, baryon_mode, GravitySolver, mass_astro.","title":"GravityConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#TidalFieldConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Bundles the time-dependent MW and LMC tidal pieces. Fields: MW_tidal_field, MW_tidal_interpolate, LMC_tidal_field, uT, tidal_lookback_time, df_traj, df_traj_LMC, length_astro, uL, MW_grid, MW_Phi, spl_pot, sim_force_baryon, particles_LMC, sim_traj_LMC.","title":"TidalFieldConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#3.-Initial-condition-structures","page":"Configs","text":"","title":"3. Initial-condition structures"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#InitialConditionsConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Drives generate_initial_conditions. Fields: model, baryon_mode, Np, pids, bulk_perturb, bulk_size, bulk_shift_size, bulk_center_size, reset_velocity, static, FDM_mass_ratio, FDM_radius_ratio, rotational_ratio, velocity_ratio, rotational_ratio_baryon, velocity_ratio_baryon, velocity_falling, MW_disk_RC, GravitySolver, SofteningLength.\n\nThe current defaults are bulk_size = 8 and SofteningLength = 0.1u\"kpc\"; the Crater II runs in the paper used bulk_size = 4.","title":"InitialConditionsConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#DensityProfileConfig","page":"Configs","text":"The full set of baryonic and halo profile parameters. Fields: baryon_β, baryon_ρ0, baryon_r0, halo_β, halo_ρ0, halo_r0, halo_α, halo_γ, halo_Q, stellar_TotalMass, stellar_ScaleRadius, thickness_ratio_stellar, gases_TotalMass, gases_ScaleRadius, thickness_ratio_gases.","title":"DensityProfileConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#MassRadiusConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Simple radial cutoffs used by MOND / RAR diagnostics. Fields: massRadius, minR, maxR.","title":"MassRadiusConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#4.-Visualization-structures","page":"Configs","text":"","title":"4. Visualization structures"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#VisualizationConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Controls the live GLMakie figure. Fields: title, suffix, size, StepsBetweenSnapshots, Realtime, dynamic_colorrange, plot_virial, plot_optical, plot_wavedm.","title":"VisualizationConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#VisualizationData","page":"Configs","text":"Pre-allocated observables for the per-step update. Fields: rho, rho_max_id, total_halo_mass, radii, r_mass_center, target_profile_model, target_profile_error.","title":"VisualizationData"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#5.-Best-fit-extraction-structures","page":"Configs","text":"","title":"5. Best-fit extraction structures"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#ProfileFitConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Holds the target density profile (gNFW / Zhao / NFW) and its 1σ uncertainties. Fields: target_profile_ρ0, target_profile_ρ0_u, target_profile_ρ0_d, target_profile_rs, target_profile_rs_u, target_profile_rs_d, target_profile_α, target_profile_α_u, target_profile_α_d, target_profile_β, target_profile_β_u, target_profile_β_d, target_profile_γ, target_profile_γ_u, target_profile_γ_d, target_fitting_rs_ratio, uniform_interval.","title":"ProfileFitConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#RCFitConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Rotation-curve fit and inner-slope beta^* configuration. Fields: target_beta_star, target_beta_star_u, target_beta_star_d, target_beta_star_r_min, target_beta_star_r_max, beta_star_error_threshold, Galaxy_i.","title":"RCFitConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#BestFitConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Time-averaging and extraction window. Fields: extract_dwarf_granule, extract_min_t, extract_mode, folder_data, average, average_start_t, average_snapshots, average_all, average_all_start_t.","title":"BestFitConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"api/configs/#6.-Usage-pattern","page":"Configs","text":"The structures are constructed implicitly by simulate_waveDM from its keyword arguments. If you are calling SPE3D_waveDM directly you can either pass the same keyword arguments or build the structures by hand. For example:\n\nusing WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nconfig_units = AstroUnitsConfig(\n    uconvert(u\"kpc\",  (8π*(C.h/2π)^2 / (3*mₐ^2 * C.H^2 * Ωₘ₀))^0.25),\n    uconvert(u\"Gyr\",  (3*C.H^2*Ωₘ₀ / (8π))^-0.5),\n    # ...\n)\n\nconfig_device = DeviceConfig(true, cu, collect)         # GPU\nconfig_tidal  = TidalFieldConfig(false, true, false,    # no MW / LMC tidal\n                                 1.0, 0.0u\"Gyr\",\n                                 DataFrame(), DataFrame(),\n                                 1.0, 1.0,\n                                 nothing, nothing, nothing,\n                                 nothing, nothing, nothing)\n\n# ... then pass these into a custom KDK driver.\n\nThe keyword-argument interface is the recommended path for users; the structs are most useful for advanced callers who want to encapsulate a \"campaign\" of related runs in a single object.","title":"6. Usage pattern"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.AstroUnitsConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Astrophysical units configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.AstroUnitsConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.DeviceConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Thin compatibility wrapper around ParallelBackend so that the existing call sites (config_device.gpu, config_device.DeviceArray, config_device.DA) keep working. The preferred way to construct a backend is now select_backend, which picks the best kind (:serial / :threads / :distributed / :gpu) automatically.\n\nFields\n\nbackend::ParallelBackend  The actual backend object.\nDeviceArray::Function     Convenience alias of backend.device_array\nDA::Function              Convenience alias of backend.distribute_array\n\nConstructors:\n\nDeviceConfig(; gpu=false, distributed=false, kind=:auto, ...)\nBuild via auto-detection.  Accepts the same kwargs as select_backend.\nDeviceConfig(gpu::Bool, device_array, distribute_array)\nBackward-compat: build a minimal serial/GPU-style config without invoking auto-detection.  The new code should prefer the keyword constructor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.DeviceConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.SimulationGrid","page":"Configs","text":"Grid and coordinate configuration for simulations.\n\nFields\n\nXmax, Ymax, Zmax: Maximum grid coordinates in each dimension\nNx, Ny, Nz: Number of grid points in each dimension\nΔ: Grid spacing in each dimension\nx, y, z: 1D coordinate arrays\nxxx, yyy, zzz: 3D coordinate arrays for each dimension\nr: 3D radial distance array\noneMatrix: 3D matrix of ones with size (Nx, Ny, Nz)\nunit_cell_volumn: Volume of a single grid cell\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.SimulationGrid"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.TimeStepConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Time step configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.TimeStepConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.TidalFieldConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Tidal field configuration for kick step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.TidalFieldConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.InitialConditionsConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Initial conditions configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.InitialConditionsConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.MassRadiusConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Mass radius configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.MassRadiusConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.VisualizationConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Visualization configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.VisualizationConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.VisualizationData","page":"Configs","text":"Visualization data configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.VisualizationData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.BestFitConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Best fit extraction configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.BestFitConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.ProfileFitConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Profile fitting configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.ProfileFitConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/configs/#WaveDM.RCFitConfig","page":"Configs","text":"Rotation curve fitting configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.RCFitConfig"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Quickstart","page":"Quickstart","text":"This page is for first-time users. If you already know what simulate_waveDM is and you want to see all the knobs, jump straight to Examples or Algorithms.","title":"Quickstart"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#0-·-Sanity-check-the-install-(30-s)","page":"Quickstart","text":"julia> using WaveDM\njulia> WaveDM.print_catalog()\n\nIf you see a table of supported models and ultra-faint dwarf galaxies, the install worked. If you see a Package WaveDM not found, go back to Installation.","title":"0 · Sanity-check the install (30 s)"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#1-·-What's-in-WaveDM.jl-—-pick-your-model","page":"Quickstart","text":"Before you write any code, run:\n\njulia> list_supported_models()\n\nYou'll get back a list of every model = :... keyword accepted by simulate_waveDM, with three useful columns:\n\nkind meaning\n\"halo\" big dark-matter halo (Milky-Way analogue, SPARC later)\n\"dwarf\" dwarf galaxy halo (Crater II, Draco, …)\n\"cluster\" galaxy cluster\n\"elliptical\" early-type galaxy\n\nEach entry also lists the allowed baryon_mode values. If you don't know what baryon_mode is, see Concepts below.","title":"1 · What's in WaveDM.jl — pick your model"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#2-·-Pick-a-galaxy-(or-stay-generic)","page":"Quickstart","text":"If you want a named galaxy (Crater II, Draco, Fornax, …), use the ultra-faint-dwarf preset:\n\njulia> list_galaxies()        # also accessible as WaveDM.galaxies()\n\nThis prints every Galaxy_id you can plug into simulate_waveDM(; model = :dwarf_UFDs, Galaxy_id = N).\n\nIf you want a custom halo (any density profile, any parameters), skip the preset and use one of the generic models:\n\nIf you want to … Use this model\ntry a Milky-Way-sized halo :MW\ntry a dwarf galaxy with a generic gNFW :dwarf\ntry a classical NFW dwarf (β = 1) :dwarf_NFW\ntry a Zhao (1996) profile (free α, β, γ) :dwarf_Zhao\ntry an early-type galaxy :Elliptical\ntry a galaxy cluster :cluster_NFW or :cluster_Burkert","title":"2 · Pick a galaxy (or stay generic)"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#3-·-The-shortest-script-that-actually-runs","page":"Quickstart","text":"Copy-paste this into a file crater2.jl and run julia --project crater2.jl:\n\nusing WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model            = :dwarf_UFDs,\n    Galaxy_id        = 6,                    # Crater II\n    baryon_mode      = :ignored,             # no baryons in this preset\n    mₐ               = 50 * 1e-22 * 1.783e-36u\"kg\",  # m₂₂ = 50\n    V                = (x, y, z, ψ) -> 0.0,  # no external potential\n    Nx               = 128,                  # try 384 for production\n    Xmax             = 20u\"kpc\",             # half-side length\n    Tmax             = 1.0u\"Gyr\",            # simulation duration\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    gpu              = false,                # set true on a GPU machine\n    Realtime         = false,                # set true if you have a monitor\n    title            = \"MyFirstRun\",\n    outputdir        = \"results/MyFirstRun\",\n)\n\nWhat this does: builds a wave-CDM Crater II halo (gNFW, β = 1, r_s = 3 kpc, Hayashi 2023 defaults), evolves it for 1 Gyr on a 128³ Cartesian grid in a 40 kpc box, and writes a snapshot series plus a CSV of total mass and mass-fraction radii to results/MyFirstRun/. Virial energies are added to the CSV when plot_virial = true.","title":"3 · The shortest script that actually runs"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#4-·-baryon_mode-in-one-line","page":"Quickstart","text":"baryon_mode tells WaveDM.jl how to handle the non-dark-matter mass in your system:\n\nbaryon_mode What it does When to use\n:ignored No baryons at all. DM-only halos (Crater II, isolated dwarfs).\n:mesh Baryons placed on the same grid as the wave field. Bulges, ICM, simple extended baryons.\n:particles_static Baryons as N-body particles, frozen during the SPE loop. Realistic MW-like host potentials.\n:particles_dynamic Baryons as N-body particles, co-evolving with the wave field. Full coupling, but expensive (~10× more).\n\nUse :ignored until you have a reason not to. Each model only accepts a subset — list_supported_models() tells you which.","title":"4 · baryon_mode in one line"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#5-·-Common-beginner-mistakes","page":"Quickstart","text":"\"Unknown model: :dwarf_UFDs\" → Make sure you're on the latest release of WaveDM.jl. If the model name is one of :SPARC_LTGs / :SPARC_Xray_ETGs / :SPARC_rotating_ETGs, those are planned but not implemented yet.\n\"Galaxy_id = 5 is out of range\" → Galaxy_id is the 1-based row index into the Hayashi 2023 UFD table (Crater II is row 6).  Run list_galaxies() to see valid values.  For other dwarfs you can use model = :dwarf_NFW / :dwarf_Zhao and pass config_profile = ..., or use model = :dwarf_UFDs with Galaxy_id = 6 for Crater II.\n\"Out of memory\" at Nx = 384 → That's a 384³ complex array ≈ 4 GB on CPU. Drop to Nx = 192, or move to a GPU.\nNothing happens for a long time at startup → simulate_waveDM is building initial conditions (gNFW sampling + FFT-Poisson solve). Watch the @info lines; if you don't see them, your call didn't dispatch — check model = :... spelling.\nBaryon particles complain about Np → The model needs you to pass Np for particle counts. list_supported_models() shows needs_Np = yes/no.","title":"5 · Common beginner mistakes"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#6-·-Where-to-go-next","page":"Quickstart","text":"Examples — 10 worked scripts, from a 30-second smoke test to Crater II with the MW + LMC tidal field.\nAlgorithms — the math behind the SPE, the KDK integrator, the resolution criteria.\nConfigs — every keyword argument of simulate_waveDM.","title":"6 · Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"This page walks through the end-to-end simulations discussed in §3 of the accompanying paper (Meng & Dong 2026). Each example is a complete, runnable Julia script. Run from the Julia REPL or julia --project with the indicated packages installed.\n\ntip: Before you start\nIf you haven't run WaveDM.jl before, skim the Quickstart page first — it shows you how to introspect the package with list_supported_models() / list_galaxies() / print_catalog() so that you know which model = :... and Galaxy_id = N combinations are supported before you write a 50-line script.","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#0.-Smoke-test-(CPU,-64)","page":"Examples","text":"The fastest way to verify that WaveDM.jl is correctly installed and that the SPE loop is wired up. Runs in a few seconds on any machine.\n\nusing WaveDM\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model = :MW,\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,          # skip baryon sampling for the smoke test\n    Xmax = 2.0,\n    Tmax = 0.001,\n    Nt   = 3,\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 64,\n    gpu = false,\n    Realtime = false,\n    unicode_plot = true,             # terminal progress\n    outputdir = mktempdir(),\n)\n\nYou should see several @info lines, a terminal heat-map of the density slice, and no errors.","title":"0. Smoke test (CPU, 64³)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#1.-Isolated-wave-DM-halo-(free-propagation,-Gaussian-IC)","page":"Examples","text":"Useful for verifying the KDK integrator in the simplest possible setup — no self-gravity, no baryons.\n\nusing WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model = :MW,\n    IC_vel = (x, y, z) -> exp(-(x^2 + y^2 + z^2) / 0.1^2),\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,\n    V     = (x, y, z, ψ) -> 0.0,\n    κ     = 0,\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 128,\n    Xmax = 2.0,\n    Tmax = 0.01,\n    Nt   = 200,\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    gpu = false,\n    Realtime = true,\n    title = \"FreeProp\",\n    outputdir = \"results/FreeProp\",\n)\n\nThe wave packet disperses under the kinetic operator; with autoset_timestep = true the timestep is chosen from the CFL criterion.","title":"1. Isolated wave-DM halo (free propagation, Gaussian IC)"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#2.-Milky-Way-ultra-faint-dwarf-—-Crater-II","page":"Examples","text":"The flagship case study from §3 of the paper. Crater II is modelled with a gNFW density profile:\n\nrho_rm DM(r) = fracrho_0(rr_s)^beta (1 + rr_s)^3-beta\n\nwith the Crater-II parameters from Hayashi et al. (2023). The boson mass is m_22 = 500, the resolution is 384^3, the box half-side is 20 kpc, and the run is 6 Gyr long. Baryons are neglected (Crater II is DM-dominated).\n\nusing WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model       = :dwarf_UFDs,\n    Galaxy_id   = 6,                          # Crater II\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,\n    mₐ          = 50 * 1e-22 * 1.783e-36u\"kg\",  # m₂₂ = 50\n    V           = (x, y, z, ψ) -> 0.0,\n    Nx          = 384,\n    Xmax        = 20u\"kpc\",\n    Tmax        = 6.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    absorb_coeff     = 10,                    # moderate absorbing layer\n    bulk_size   = 4,                          # value used in the paper\n    gpu         = true,\n    Realtime    = true,\n    plot_virial = true,                       # monitor virial energies\n    title       = \"CraterII\",\n    outputdir   = \"results/CraterII\",\n)\n\nThe simulation writes an overview figure, an MP4 of the live density slice, and a CSV with the time series of total mass, mass-fraction radii, and virial energies.\n\nFor production profile fitting, set target_profile_model = :dwarf_Zhao and pass the Crater II target_profile_ρ0, target_profile_rs, target_profile_α, target_profile_β, and target_profile_γ values from AstroIC.load_data_UFDs(). The short script above uses the built-in Crater II initial profile, but the default target profile is the Milky Way one.","title":"2. Milky Way ultra-faint dwarf — Crater II"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#2.1-Crater-II-with-the-Milky-Way-tidal-field","page":"Examples","text":"To add a time-dependent MW tidal field (and, optionally, the LMC perturbation) to the same halo:\n\nusing WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model       = :dwarf_UFDs,\n    Galaxy_id   = 6,\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,\n    mₐ          = 50 * 1e-22 * 1.783e-36u\"kg\",\n    V           = (x, y, z, ψ) -> 0.0,\n    Nx          = 384,\n    Xmax        = 20u\"kpc\",\n    Tmax        = 6.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    absorb_coeff     = 10,\n    bulk_size   = 4,\n    MW_tidal_field = true,                    # MW tidal field\n    df_traj     = df_traj,                    # from the lookback step in §4\n    tidal_lookback_time = 6.0u\"Gyr\",\n    LMC_tidal_field = true,                   # LMC perturbation\n    gpu         = true,\n    title       = \"CraterII_MW_LMC\",\n    outputdir   = \"results/CraterII_MW_LMC\",\n)\n\nThe halo ends up in a tide-driven non-equilibrium state — the radially averaged velocity dispersion no longer matches the Jeans prediction, which is a useful diagnostic for stripping studies (see Algorithms §6.3).","title":"2.1 Crater II with the Milky Way tidal field"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#3.-Sampling-the-Milky-Way-as-a-static-N-body-background","page":"Examples","text":"If you want to drive a wave-CDM simulation with an explicit N-body representation of the MW, sample the baryons with AstroIC and pass them in via baryon_mode = :particles_static:\n\nusing WaveDM\nusing AstroIC\nusing PhysicalParticles, UnitfulAstro, Unitful\n\n# Thin stellar disk\ndisk_thin = generate(\n    AstroIC.ExponentialDisc(;\n        collection       = STAR,\n        NumSamples       = 50_000,\n        TotalMass        = 3.5e10u\"Msun\",\n        ScaleRadius      = 2.42u\"kpc\",\n        ScaleHeight      = 0.30u\"kpc\",\n    )\n)\n\n# HI gas disk\ngas_HI = generate(\n    AstroIC.ExponentialDisc(;\n        collection       = GAS,\n        NumSamples       = 30_000,\n        TotalMass        = 8.0e9u\"Msun\",\n        ScaleRadius      = 7.0u\"kpc\",\n        ScaleHeight      = 0.085u\"kpc\",\n        HoleRadius       = 4.0u\"kpc\",         # R_m in the Zhu 2023 model\n    )\n)\n\n# Add a bulge, thick disk, etc. — see the paper for the full MW model\nparticles = [disk_thin; gas_HI]\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model              = :MW,\n    baryon_mode        = :particles_static,\n    baryon_particles   = particles,\n    GravitySolver      = Tree(),\n    SofteningLength    = 0.1u\"kpc\",           # code default; ε ~ mean particle spacing\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 256,\n    Xmax = 100u\"kpc\",\n    Tmax = 3.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    gpu = true,\n    title = \"MW_static_Nbody\",\n    outputdir = \"results/MW_static_Nbody\",\n)\n\nbaryon_mode = :particles_static means the MW particles are held fixed — their gravitational potential is evaluated at every SPE step but they do not move. This is the cheapest way to add a realistic host potential.","title":"3. Sampling the Milky Way as a static N-body background"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#4.-Trajectory-lookback-of-Crater-II","page":"Examples","text":"AstroIC.load_data_MW_satellites() returns the Battaglia et al. (2022) observational catalogue. The script below integrates the Crater II orbit backward 6 Gyr in a time-dependent MW potential, with the LMC perturbation included. The resulting trajectory can then be fed to the wave-DM simulation as tidal_initial_pos / tidal_initial_vel / df_traj.\n\nusing AstroNbodySim, AstroIC, WaveDM\nusing DataFrames, Unitful, PhysicalParticles\nusing StructArrays\n\n# 1. Load the catalogue and pick Crater II\ndf = AstroIC.load_data_MW_satellites()\nid = findfirst(df.Galaxy .== \"Crater II\")\n\n# 2. Convert observables → Galactocentric initial state\npos = PVector(df.X[id], df.Y[id], df.Z[id], u\"kpc\")\nvel = -PVector(df.v_X[id], df.v_Y[id], df.v_Z[id], u\"km/s\")\n\n# 3. Build a one-particle N-body system\nCrater_II = StructArray([Star(uAstro; id = 1)])\nCrater_II.Pos[1] = pos\nCrater_II.Vel[1] = vel\n\n# 4. Backward integration in a (time-dependent) MW potential\nfunction MW_bg_force(particle, t)\n    acc_DM = acc_from_MW_halo(particle)         # user-defined MW DM acceleration\n    acc_b  = acc_from_MW_baryons(particle)      # user-defined MW disk+bulge\n    return acc_DM + acc_b\nend\n\nsim_traj = Simulation(deepcopy(Crater_II);\n    bgforce   = Function[MW_bg_force],\n    TimeEnd   = 6.0u\"Gyr\",\n    TimeStep  = 0.0u\"Gyr\",                       # adaptive\n)\nrun(sim_traj)\n\n# 5. The orbit is in `sim_traj.saveresult` — pass it as df_traj to a wave-DM run.\n\nThe full MW background force should include the disk, bulge, and dark-matter halo (Zhu et al. 2023 form). See examples/03_lookback.jl in the repository for a complete working version with the LMC perturbation.","title":"4. Trajectory lookback of Crater II"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#5.-Self-interacting-wave-DM","page":"Examples","text":"Set kappa neq 0 to add a contact self-interaction V = kappapsi^2. WaveDM.jl supports repulsive (kappa  0) and attractive (kappa  0) regimes.\n\nusing WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model = :MW,\n    IC_vel = (x, y, z) -> exp(-(x^2 + y^2 + z^2) / 0.1^2),\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,\n    V     = (x, y, z, ψ) -> 0.0,\n    κ     = 50.0,                               # repulsive self-interaction\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 128,\n    Xmax = 2.0,\n    Tmax = 0.05,\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    gpu = true,\n    title = \"SelfInteracting\",\n    outputdir = \"results/SelfInteracting\",\n)\n\nFor kappa  0 the auto-timestep includes the term 2pi  (kappamaxpsi^2) which limits phase variations from the self-interaction.","title":"5. Self-interacting wave DM"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#6.-Custom-potential-—-cross-disciplinary-use","page":"Examples","text":"The pluggable V(x, y, z, ψ) interface lets WaveDM.jl solve any local nonlinear Schrödinger problem. For example, a 1D effective optical lattice:\n\nusing WaveDM\n\nV_optical(x, y, z, ψ) = 1000 * sin(2π * x)^2 + 1000 * sin(2π * y)^2\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model = :MW,\n    IC_vel = (x, y, z) -> exp(-(x^2 + y^2 + z^2) / 0.05^2) * exp(im * 50 * x),\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,\n    V     = V_optical,\n    κ     = 5000.0,                             # strong Kerr nonlinearity\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 128,\n    Xmax = 1.0,\n    Tmax = 0.01,\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    gpu = true,\n    title = \"OpticalLattice\",\n    outputdir = \"results/OpticalLattice\",\n)\n\nThis makes WaveDM.jl directly usable for nonlinear-optics and BEC problems without modifying the source.","title":"6. Custom potential — cross-disciplinary use"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#7.-Multi-level-parallel-execution","page":"Examples","text":"","title":"7. Multi-level parallel execution"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#7.1-Multi-threaded-FFTW-on-a-CPU-node","page":"Examples","text":"julia -t 16 my_script.jl\n\nusing FFTW\nusing WaveDM\n\nFFTW.set_provider!(\"mkl\")                      # or \"fftw\" — must precede the first FFT\nFFTW.set_num_threads(Threads.nthreads())\n\nsimulate_waveDM(; Nx = Ny = Nz = 384, gpu = false, ...)  # no extra keyword needed","title":"7.1 Multi-threaded FFTW on a CPU node"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#7.2-Distributed-run-across-multiple-cores-/-nodes","page":"Examples","text":"warning: Warning\nThe distributed FFT Poisson solver is not implemented in the current release. Distributed workers can accelerate initial-condition generation and N-body work, but the full SPE loop must run on a single process.\n\nusing Distributed\naddprocs(8)\n@everywhere using WaveDM\n\n# Distributed-memory IC generation (SPE loop remains single-process)\nψ_IC = simulate_waveDM(;\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 256,\n    Xmax = 5.0,\n    distributed_memory = true,\n    gpu = false,\n)\n\nFor multi-node runs, replace addprocs(8) with a cluster manager, e.g.\n\nusing Distributed\naddprocs([(\"node1.example.com\", :auto), (\"node2.example.com\", :auto)])\n@everywhere using WaveDM","title":"7.2 Distributed run across multiple cores / nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#7.3-GPU-run","page":"Examples","text":"using WaveDM\nusing CUDA\n@assert CUDA.functional()\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 512,\n    Xmax = 5.0,\n    Tmax = 5.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    gpu = true,\n    Realtime = true,\n)","title":"7.3 GPU run"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#8.-Re-using-initial-conditions-across-runs","page":"Examples","text":"simulate_waveDM(; IC_vel = path) accepts a JLD2 path that was written by a previous save_IC = true run. This is the recommended way to scan parameters (resolution, zeta_rm rot, etc.) on a fixed initial condition.\n\n# 1. First run: build and save the IC\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model = :MW,\n    Xmax = 2.0, Tmax = 0.01, Nt = 1,           # one step is enough if we only want the IC\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 256,\n    save_IC = true,\n    outputdir = \"results/IC_run\",\n)\n# output: results/IC_run/<title>, <suffix> - IC.jld2\n\n# 2. Second run: re-use the saved IC, vary parameters\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    IC_vel = \"results/IC_run/<title>, <suffix> - IC.jld2\",\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,\n    Xmax = 2.0, Tmax = 0.5,\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    rotational_ratio = 0.0,                    # no rotation this time\n    bulk_perturb    = false,                   # disable pooling\n    gpu = true,\n    title = \"Scan_rot0\",\n    outputdir = \"results/Scan_rot0\",\n)","title":"8. Re-using initial conditions across runs"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#9.-Post-processing-—-radial-profile,-virial-energies,-mass-fraction-radii","page":"Examples","text":"simulate_waveDM returns a DataFrame dfProp with the time series of total mass, R_10 ldots R_90 mass-fraction radii, and (if plot_virial = true) the kinetic, potential, and quantum energies.\n\nusing CSV, DataFrames, GLMakie\n\nψ, fig, figMOND, chi2RC, dfProp, dfAcc, avg = simulate_waveDM(;\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 256, plot_virial = true, gpu = true,\n)\n\n# 9.1 Mass-fraction radii vs time\nfig_r = GLMakie.Figure()\nax = GLMakie.Axis(fig_r[1, 1]; ylabel = \"R (kpc)\", xlabel = \"t (Gyr)\")\nfor i in 1:9\n    GLMakie.lines!(ax, dfProp.t, dfProp[!, \"R$(i*10)_kpc\"]; label = \"$(i*10)%\")\nend\nGLMakie.axislegend(ax)\nGLMakie.save(\"radii_vs_t.png\", fig_r)\n\n# 9.2 Virial energy convergence\nfig_v = GLMakie.Figure()\nax2 = GLMakie.Axis(fig_v[1, 1]; ylabel = \"Energy\", xlabel = \"t (Gyr)\")\nGLMakie.lines!(ax2, dfProp.t, dfProp.K_total; label = \"K\")\nGLMakie.lines!(ax2, dfProp.t, dfProp.V_total; label = \"V\")\nGLMakie.lines!(ax2, dfProp.t, dfProp.Q_total; label = \"Q\")\nGLMakie.lines!(ax2, dfProp.t, dfProp.E_total; label = \"E = K+V+Q\")\nGLMakie.axislegend(ax2)\nGLMakie.save(\"virial.png\", fig_v)\n\nA halo is virialized when the total energy mathcal E = mathcal K + mathcal V + mathcal Q is approximately constant and the radii of fixed mass fractions stop growing. The paper's Crater II simulation starts at t=-6 Gyr and reaches virial equilibrium after roughly 4 Gyr of evolution, i.e. near tapprox-2 Gyr.","title":"9. Post-processing — radial profile, virial energies, mass-fraction radii"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#10.-Best-practices","page":"Examples","text":"Start small. Validate with a 64^3 CPU run before launching a 512^3 GPU production run.\nEnable autoset_timestep = true. It picks Delta t from the CFL criterion with a 10 % safety margin.\nSave initial conditions. Use save_IC = true and re-load with IC_vel = \"...\" to scan parameters on a fixed halo.\nTune absorb_coeff. Defaults of 10 are good for production. Set absorb_coeff = 0 to disable and see the boundary.\nPick the right baryon_mode. :mesh is fastest, :particles_static is more flexible, :particles_dynamic is the most realistic but most expensive.\nPick one backend for the SPE loop. The distributed FFT Poisson solver is not implemented yet, so a full run must use the serial/threads or GPU backend; distributed workers are useful for IC generation and N-body work.\nProfile the visualization overhead. If Realtime = true is the bottleneck, switch to unicode_plot = true for headless or Realtime = false for batch runs.\nCheck virialization before analyzing. Use the QSS criteria in Algorithms §6 to verify the halo is in quasi-stationary equilibrium before you fit a profile or compare with observations.\nDocument your parameters. Keep a record of Nx, Xmax, Tmax, mₐ, ζ_rot, ζ_pool, absorb_coeff, and baryon_mode for every run.","title":"10. Best practices"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Next-steps","page":"Examples","text":"The full list of keyword arguments is in Simulation.\nThe mathematical background of the KDK solver is in Algorithms.\nIf you build something useful on top of WaveDM.jl, consider opening a PR — see the contribution guide.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#Introduction","page":"Introduction","text":"","title":"Introduction"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#1.-Wave-Dark-Matter-Background","page":"Introduction","text":"Wave (or \"fuzzy\") cold dark matter describes dark matter as an ultralight bosonic field with particle masses in the range m_a sim 10^-23–10^-20 eV [Hu et al. 2000; Hui 2021]. For such small masses, the de Broglie wavelength\n\nlambda_rm dB = frachm_av\n\nreaches astrophysical scales inside galactic halos. For a typical halo velocity v sim 100 km s^-1 and m_a = 10^-22 eV, lambda_rm dB approx 12 kpc — comparable to the size of galactic substructure. Wave CDM therefore exhibits phenomena absent in particle CDM: solitonic cores supported by quantum pressure, granule structures from wave interference, halo fluctuations, and the suppression of small-scale power below lambda_rm dB.","title":"1. Wave Dark Matter Background"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#1.1-Bose–Einstein-condensation-and-the-classical-field-limit","page":"Introduction","text":"For ultralight bosons the critical temperature for Bose–Einstein condensation is extremely high, T_c sim 10^35 K, while the cosmic microwave background temperature is sim 3 K and the effective kinetic temperature of the bosons is only T_rm eff sim 10^-25 K for m_a sim 10^-22 eV. The system is therefore in the effective zero-temperature regime.\n\nIn this regime the dynamics of a highly Bose-degenerate bosonic system is well described by a single complex-valued macroscopic wavefunction psi(mathbf r t), the order parameter of the condensate. Once equilibrium is reached, almost all bosons reside in the condensate; the ground-state component of the condensate is the soliton familiar from the wave-CDM literature, while the excited component carries collective modes (phonons, vortices, localized modes).\n\nCrucially, the classical-field description is not limited to systems that have already established global phase coherence. Even far from equilibrium, when the bosons' de Broglie wavelengths overlap strongly and many low-energy modes are highly occupied, the system can still be evolved with the classical-field GPE — interpreting psi(mathbf rt) as a classical matter-wave field. This is the regime relevant for wave-CDM halos.","title":"1.1 Bose–Einstein condensation and the classical-field limit"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#1.2-The-Schrödinger–Poisson-system","page":"Introduction","text":"The evolution of psi is governed by the Gross–Pitaevskii equation (GPE), coupled self-consistently with the Poisson equation of gravity in the case of DM halos:\n\nbeginaligned\nihbarpartial_t psi = -frachbar^22mnabla^2psi + mPhi_rm totalpsi \nnabla^2 Phi_rm total = 4pi Gbiglrho_rm DM + rho_rm baryonbigr\nendaligned\n\nwith rho_rm DM = mpsi^2 and Phi_rm total the total gravitational potential.\n\nApplying the Madelung transformation psi = sqrtrhome^iShbar yields a quantum hydrodynamic form: a continuity equation and a quantum Bernoulli equation, where the quantum potential\n\nQ = -frachbar^22mfracnabla^2sqrtrhosqrtrho\n\nencodes the Heisenberg uncertainty principle. The corresponding quantum pressure -(1m)nabla Q enters the quantum Euler equation.\n\nA density-weighted volume integral of Q defines the quantum (gradient) energy\n\nmathcalQ equiv int rho Qd^3r = frachbar^22mint nablasqrtrho^2 d^3r\n\nwhere the surface term vanishes for any realistic NFW-like halo. The total energy mathcalE = mathcalK + mathcalV + mathcalQ is approximately conserved for a virialized, isolated halo. In a simulation with a fixed baryonic background or an absorbing boundary, mathcalE drifts as mass and energy leave the domain; this drift is a physical effect of the setup, not a numerical artefact.","title":"1.2 The Schrödinger–Poisson system"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#1.3-Dimensionless-units","page":"Introduction","text":"Following Edwards et al. (2018) and Glennon et al. (2021), WaveDM.jl adopts the characteristic units\n\nbeginaligned\nmathcalL = left(frac8pihbar^23m^2 H_0^2Omega_m_0right)^14\n            approx 121left(frac10^-23mathrmeVmright)^12mathrmkpc \nmathcalT = left(frac8pi3H_0^2Omega_m_0right)^12\n            approx 755mathrmGyr \nmathcalM = frac1Gleft(frac8pi3H_0^2Omega_m_0right)^-14\n            left(frachbarmright)^32\n            approx 7times10^7left(frac10^-23mathrmeVmright)^32M_odot\nendaligned\n\nIn these units the SPE takes the compact form\n\nbeginaligned\nipartial_t psi = -tfrac12nabla^2 psi + Phi_rm totalpsi \nnabla^2 Phi_rm total = 4pibigl(rho_rm DM + rho_rm baryonbigr)\nendaligned\n\nwith psi = sqrtrhoe^itheta and mathbf v = nablatheta. The self-interaction term kappapsi^2psi, with kappa = 4pihbara_s(mathcalT m^2), can be added to model repulsive (kappa  0) or attractive (kappa  0) boson-boson contact interactions. WaveDM.jl supports kappa = 0 (non-interacting), kappa  0, and kappa  0 regimes.","title":"1.3 Dimensionless units"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#1.4-A-generalized-nonlinear-Schrödinger-interface","page":"Introduction","text":"For cross-disciplinary use, WaveDM.jl solves the generalized nonlinear Schrödinger equation (GNLSE)\n\nipartial_t psi(mathbf r t) = -tfrac12nabla^2psi + V(mathbf r t psi)psi\n\nwhere V is an arbitrary user-supplied potential. Two classes of V are supported natively:\n\nLocal interactions — V = F(psi) depending pointwise on the field amplitude. Example: the Kerr nonlinearity V = kappapsi^2 for self-interacting wave CDM and instantaneous nonlinear optical response.\nNonlocal interactions — V = int U(mathbf r - mathbf r)psi(mathbf r)^2 d^3 r, or equivalently the solution of an auxiliary field equation such as the Poisson equation. Example: the self-gravitational potential in wave CDM, dipole–dipole interactions in atomic condensates, thermal-optical nonlinearities in nonlinear optics.\n\nThis interface is the foundation of WaveDM.jl's cross-disciplinary applicability: astrophysics, nonlinear optics, and cold-atom physics all reduce to choosing an appropriate V — the same code base, the same solver, the same parallel infrastructure.","title":"1.4 A generalized nonlinear Schrödinger interface"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#2.-Design-Philosophy","page":"Introduction","text":"The primary design goal of WaveDM.jl is to make galaxy-scale simulations accessible to a broad range of astrophysicists who are not necessarily computational specialists, while remaining a community-oriented open-source project that advanced users and developers can extend without mastering the entire codebase.","title":"2. Design Philosophy"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#2.1-Adaptability-for-general-users","page":"Introduction","text":"Wave dark matter simulations typically demand expertise across numerical methods for the Schrödinger equation, parallel computing, initial conditions, N-body gravity, and astrophysical analysis. WaveDM.jl unifies these components behind a small set of high-level keyword arguments.\n\nA typical user might want to:\n\nvary the boson mass m_a and self-interaction strength kappa to explore different wave CDM scenarios;\nenable or disable tidal perturbations from the LMC or a host halo;\nchange numerical resolution, the initial halo, or the velocity-field configuration.\n\nIn every case the user adjusts a few keywords — they do not edit the underlying implementation. This is enabled by:\n\nthe generalized SPE formulation V(mathbf r t psi)psi, which exposes a single pluggable potential interface;\nthe galaxy-simulation toolbox (Section 3) with ready-to-use initial conditions, trajectory lookback, tidal fields, and real-time visualization.","title":"2.1 Adaptability for general users"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#2.2-Extensibility-for-advanced-users","page":"Introduction","text":"WaveDM.jl is built on the JuliaAstroSim ecosystem of reusable components. New physical models — additional baryonic feedback mechanisms, SPH for gas dynamics, etc. — can be added by implementing a small set of well-defined interfaces. The shared infrastructure includes:\n\nPhysicalParticles.jl — fundamental data structures and vector algebra;\nAstroIO.jl — file I/O;\nAstroIC.jl — initial conditions;\nAstroPlot.jl — analysis and visualization;\nPhysicalTrees.jl and PhysicalMeshes.jl — tree and mesh gravity solvers;\nPhysicalFDM.jl and PhysicalFFT.jl — FDM and FFT solvers for PDEs;\nAstroNbodySim.jl — N-body integration, glue layer, and parallel runtime.\n\nBy reusing these packages, developers can contribute new features without re-implementing data structures, parallel I/O, or visualization plumbing. This makes the codebase approachable for new contributors and sustainable over the long term.","title":"2.2 Extensibility for advanced users"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#3.-Key-Features-of-WaveDM.jl","page":"Introduction","text":"","title":"3. Key Features of WaveDM.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#3.1-Coupled-wave-N-body-evolution","page":"Introduction","text":"A pseudo-spectral split-step Fourier solver for the 3D time-dependent SPE (§3 of the paper).\nBaryonic components can be placed on the mesh (baryon_mode = :mesh) or as N-body particles (baryon_mode = :particles_static / :particles_dynamic).\nParticle-based gravity supports both DirectSum (mathcalO(N^2)) and Tree (mathcalO(Nlog N), Gadget-2 scheme) solvers from AstroNbodySim.jl.\nTime-dependent tidal forces from the Milky Way (MW_tidal_field = true) and from external perturbers like the LMC (LMC_tidal_field = true) can be added.\nBaryons can be co-evolved with the wave field (bidirectional Poisson coupling).","title":"3.1 Coupled wave + N-body evolution"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#3.2-Multi-level-parallel-execution","page":"Introduction","text":"Multi-threading — launch Julia with julia -t N; multi-threaded FFTW can be enabled with FFTW.set_provider!(\"mkl\"); FFTW.set_num_threads(Threads.nthreads()).\nDistributed memory — set distributed_memory = true and addprocs(N); WaveDM.jl uses Julia's native Distributed.jl (TCP/IP transport), with future MPI support planned. The distributed FFT Poisson solver is not implemented yet, so the full SPE loop currently runs on a single process.\nGPU acceleration — set gpu = true; WaveDM.jl dispatches to CUDA.jl for FFT and matrix-heavy work.\n\nThe same script runs unchanged across CPU, multi-threaded, and GPU configurations; multi-node execution currently applies to initial-condition generation and N-body work.","title":"3.2 Multi-level parallel execution"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#3.3-Galaxy-simulation-toolbox","page":"Introduction","text":"Initial conditions for gNFW / NFW / Zhao profiles, SPARC late-type galaxies, SPARC early-type galaxies, dwarf spheroidals (e.g. Crater II), and the Milky Way.\nTrajectory lookback of MW satellites from Battaglia et al. (2022) with optional LMC perturbations.\nTidal field evaluation at the satellite's instantaneous position along its lookback orbit, with the mass-weighted mean (center-of-mass) acceleration subtracted to remove spurious center-of-mass motion.\nVirial diagnostics — total kinetic, potential, and quantum energies; radii enclosing 10 %–90 % mass fractions; velocity dispersion.\nReal-time visualization via GLMakie.jl (Realtime = true) and headless UnicodePlots.jl (unicode_plot = true).","title":"3.3 Galaxy-simulation toolbox"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#4.-Project-Ecosystem","page":"Introduction","text":"WaveDM.jl is part of the JuliaAstroSim ecosystem, which provides a comprehensive set of tools for astrophysical simulations:\n\nPackage Role\nAstroSimBase.jl Basic type definitions and unit conventions\nPhysicalParticles.jl N-body particle types and vector algebra\nPhysicalFFT.jl FFT solvers for physical problems\nPhysicalFDM.jl Finite-difference solvers for PDEs\nPhysicalMeshes.jl Mesh-based gravity solvers\nAstroNbodySim.jl N-body simulations, glue layer, parallel runtime\nAstroIC.jl Initial condition generation\nAstroIO.jl Snapshot I/O\nAstroPlot.jl Visualization and analysis\nGalacticDynamics.jl Density profiles and orbit calculations\n\nTogether these packages form a versatile simulation stack that WaveDM.jl builds on for the SPE, N-body, and visualization layers.","title":"4. Project Ecosystem"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#5.-Cross-Disciplinary-Applications","page":"Introduction","text":"Beyond astrophysics, the GNLSE interface and the pluggable potential V let WaveDM.jl simulate a range of nonlinear Schrödinger systems:\n\nNonlinear optics — Kerr and thermal-optical nonlinearities, beam propagation in waveguides.\nCold-atom physics — Bose–Einstein condensates, vortex dynamics, dipole–dipole interactions.\nPlasma physics — non-relativistic wave-kinetic models.\n\nAstrophysics, nonlinear optics, and condensed-matter physics thus share the same numerical infrastructure, enabling inter-disciplinary studies at no extra implementation cost.","title":"5. Cross-Disciplinary Applications"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#6.-When-WaveDM.jl-is-the-right-tool","page":"Introduction","text":"✓ You want a galaxy-scale simulation that couples a wave CDM halo to baryons, with a time-dependent tidal field.\n✓ You want to look back the orbit of a MW satellite and run a wave-CDM simulation under a live host potential.\n✓ You want the same code to run on a laptop, a workstation with a GPU, or a multi-node cluster.\n✓ You want a modular framework that supports general nonlinear Schrödinger problems.\n\nFor purely cosmological wave-CDM simulations, dedicated cosmological codes (e.g. UltraDark.jl, GAMER) are more appropriate; WaveDM.jl focuses on galactic dynamics.","title":"6. When WaveDM.jl is the right tool"},{"category":"section","location":"introduction/#7.-Choosing-a-model-—-a-one-page-decision-tree","page":"Introduction","text":"The model = :... keyword is the first decision every user makes. If you are not sure which one to use, work through this section top-to-bottom.\n\n<style>\n.model-card { border: 1px solid #ddd; border-radius: 6px; padding: 1em 1.2em; margin: 0.8em 0; background: #fafafa; }\n.model-card h4 { margin: 0 0 0.4em 0; }\n.model-card code { background: #eef; padding: 1px 5px; border-radius: 3px; }\n.model-card ul { margin: 0.3em 0 0 0; padding-left: 1.4em; }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"model-card\"> <h4>🌌 I want a Milky-Way-sized halo with a realistic baryonic potential</h4> Use <code>model = :MW</code> with <code>baryonmode = :mesh</code> for a fast calculation that resolves the bulge + discs on the same grid, or <code>baryonmode = :particles_static</code> if you want the standard Zhu (2023) mass model sampled as 50 k–100 k N-body particles. Crater II satellites under a live MW tidal field live here. </div>\n\n<div class=\"model-card\"> <h4>🌑 I want a Crater II / Draco / ultra-faint dwarf simulation</h4> Use <code>model = :dwarfUFDs</code>, then pick <code>Galaxyid</code> from <code>listgalaxies()</code>. Right now only <code>Galaxyid = 6</code> (Crater II) is wired up to a published halo; for the others, fall back to <code>:dwarfNFW</code> with custom <code>haloρ0</code> / <code>halo_r0</code>. </div>\n\n<div class=\"model-card\"> <h4>🪐 I want a generic dwarf halo with my own parameters</h4> Use <code>model = :dwarfNFW</code> (β = 1), <code>:dwarfZhao</code> (free α, β, γ), or <code>:dwarf</code> (gNFW + optional stellar disc). </div>\n\n<div class=\"model-card\"> <h4>🌠 I want an early-type galaxy or a galaxy cluster</h4> Use <code>:Elliptical</code> (gNFW halo + Jaffe stellar profile), <code>:clusterNFW</code> (gNFW + β-model ICM), or <code>:clusterBurkert</code> (Burkert halo + β-model ICM). </div>\n\n<div class=\"model-card\"> <h4>🧪 I want a non-astrophysics Schrödinger problem (NLO, BEC, …)</h4> Use <code>model = :MW</code> with a custom <code>IC_vel = (x, y, z) -> …</code> and a custom <code>V = (x, y, z, ψ) -> …</code>. See Examples for the optical-lattice demo. </div>\n\nAfter you have picked the model, run\n\nlist_supported_models()\n\nand look at the baryon_mode column to know which baryon_mode values the model accepts. The full matrix is also exposed programmatically — see the print_catalog() helper for a one-liner cheatsheet.","title":"7. Choosing a model — a one-page decision tree"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installation","page":"Installation","text":"","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Prerequisites","page":"Installation","text":"WaveDM.jl requires Julia 1.12 or later (the CI suite runs on Julia 1.12.6). Download Julia from the official website and verify the installation:\n\njulia --version","title":"Prerequisites"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Optional:-GPU-support","page":"Installation","text":"If you plan to use GPU acceleration you will need:\n\nAn NVIDIA GPU with CUDA support.\nThe CUDA Toolkit (version 12.x recommended) and a working nvidia-smi on PATH.\nOn Windows, use the latest available NVIDIA driver that matches the CUDA toolkit bundled with CUDA.jl.","title":"Optional: GPU support"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Installation-2","page":"Installation","text":"","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#From-the-Julia-registry-(recommended)","page":"Installation","text":"Open a Julia REPL, press ] to enter package mode, then run:\n\npkg> add WaveDM","title":"From the Julia registry (recommended)"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#From-GitHub-(development-version)","page":"Installation","text":"pkg> add https://github.com/JuliaAstroSim/WaveDM.jl","title":"From GitHub (development version)"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#From-a-local-clone-(developer-workflow)","page":"Installation","text":"git clone https://github.com/JuliaAstroSim/WaveDM.jl.git\ncd WaveDM.jl\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(PackageSpec(path=\".\"))'","title":"From a local clone (developer workflow)"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Required-dependencies","page":"Installation","text":"The following packages are required at runtime. They are installed automatically by Pkg.add WaveDM, so no extra step is needed for a CPU-only installation.\n\nPackage Role\nAstroIC Initial condition generation\nAstroNbodySim N-body gravity and the parallel runtime glue\nAstroPlot Analysis and post-processing visualization\nAstroSimBase Common type and unit definitions\nPhysicalParticles N-body particle types and vector algebra\nPhysicalFFT Spectral primitives for the SPE solver\nPhysicalFDM Finite-difference operators (gradient, boundary)\nPhysicalMeshes Mesh bookkeeping used by the FFT Poisson solver\nGalacticDynamics Density profiles and orbit calculations\nFFTW CPU FFT backend\nDistributed Distributed-memory parallelism\nDataFrames, CSV, JLD2 Data I/O\nUnitful, UnitfulAstro Unit handling\nLsqFit, Optim, Dierckx Profile fitting\nObservables, StatsBase, SpecialFunctions Numerical utilities\nGLMakie, CairoMakie, UnicodePlots Visualization backends","title":"Required dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Optional-dependencies","page":"Installation","text":"Package Required for\nCUDA GPU acceleration (gpu = true)\nSundials High-order N-body integrators via AstroNbodySim\nDagger Distributed task graph (advanced)\nTullio, Zygote Macro-based and AD-accelerated kernels (advanced)\nPaddedViews, Strided Strided-array optimizations for large grids\nOffsetArrays Offset indexing for ghost-zone arrays","title":"Optional dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Setting-up-GPU-support","page":"Installation","text":"","title":"Setting up GPU support"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#1.-Install-the-CUDA-toolkit","page":"Installation","text":"Follow the CUDA.jl installation guide for your platform. On Windows:\n\nInstall the latest NVIDIA Studio / Game Ready driver.\nVerify that nvidia-smi runs and shows your GPU.\nIn the Julia REPL, run using CUDA; CUDA.functional()?true — it should print true.","title":"1. Install the CUDA toolkit"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#2.-Verify-in-Julia","page":"Installation","text":"using CUDA\n\nif CUDA.has_cuda()\n    println(\"CUDA is available\")\n    println(\"GPU:           \", CUDA.name(CUDA.device()))\n    println(\"Capability:    \", CUDA.capability(CUDA.device()))\n    println(\"Total memory:  \", CUDA.totalmemory(CUDA.device()) ÷ 1024^3, \" GiB\")\nelse\n    println(\"CUDA is not available\")\nend","title":"2. Verify in Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#3.-Smoke-test-WaveDM.jl-on-the-GPU","page":"Installation","text":"using WaveDM\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 64,\n    Nt = 3,\n    Xmax = 2.0,\n    Tmax = 0.001,\n    gpu = true,\n    Realtime = false,         # headless test\n    unicode_plot = true,      # terminal progress\n    outputdir = mktempdir(),\n)","title":"3. Smoke test WaveDM.jl on the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Enabling-high-performance-FFT","page":"Installation","text":"For multi-threaded FFTW on a CPU node:\n\nusing FFTW\nFFTW.set_provider!(\"mkl\")            # or \"fftw\" if you prefer the bundled backend\nFFTW.set_num_threads(Threads.nthreads())\n\nPair this with julia -t auto (or julia -t N) when launching Julia.","title":"Enabling high-performance FFT"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Enabling-distributed-memory","page":"Installation","text":"Add worker processes from inside your script before loading WaveDM:\n\nusing Distributed\naddprocs(4)              # spawn four local workers\n@everywhere using WaveDM\n\nthen pass distributed_memory = true to simulate_waveDM / SPE3D_waveDM. For multi-node clusters use addprocs([(\"node1\", :auto), (\"node2\", :auto), ...]) with SSH or a cluster manager.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThe distributed FFT Poisson solver is not implemented in the current release. Distributed workers currently accelerate initial-condition generation and N-body work; the full SPE loop must run on a single process.","title":"Enabling distributed memory"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Verifying-the-installation","page":"Installation","text":"pkg> test WaveDM\n\ntest WaveDM runs the unit, integration, and energy-convergence tests in test/.","title":"Verifying the installation"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Recommended-workflow","page":"Installation","text":"Use Julia ≥ 1.10 (we test against the current LTS and the latest release).\nCreate a dedicated project environment for each simulation campaign — reproducibility matters.\nLaunch with julia -t auto for multi-threading; add FFTW.set_num_threads(...) for the FFT path.\nUse GPU for grids gtrsim 256^3; switch back to CPU for very small problems where kernel launch overhead dominates.\nFor multi-node runs, always profile a small CPU run first to validate the setup before scaling out.\nKeep dependencies updated: pkg> update regularly.","title":"Recommended workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Troubleshooting","page":"Installation","text":"Symptom Likely cause / fix\nOutOfMemoryError on GPU Reduce Nx, Ny, Nz or set gpu = false\nSlow first run Julia JIT compile time — second runs are much faster; use PackageCompiler for a precompiled sysimage\nMethodError: no method matching ... ::Float32 The default V in simulate_waveDM is Float32; supply your own if you need Float64\nFFTW.PROVIDERS not picking up MKL using FFTW; FFTW.set_provider!(\"mkl\") must be called before the first FFT\nDistributed workers cannot load WaveDM Add @everywhere using WaveDM after addprocs\nCUDA initialization fails on Windows Make sure the CUDA driver matches the CUDA toolkit bundled with your CUDA.jl version\nProfile-fitting stalls Reduce extract_min_t so fewer snapshots are scanned, or pre-fit on a single snapshot\n\nIf you encounter an issue not listed here, please open a GitHub Issue with a minimal reproduction.","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Uninstallation","page":"Installation","text":"pkg> rm WaveDM","title":"Uninstallation"},{"category":"section","location":"installation/#Next-steps","page":"Installation","text":"Explore the Examples section for end-to-end simulations.\nRead the Algorithms section to understand the SPE, split-step Fourier method, boundary conditions, and QSS diagnostics.\nCheck the API reference for the configuration structures.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#References","page":"References and citations","text":"This page collects the scientific literature and software packages cited in the WaveDM.jl paper and documentation. It is meant as a quick reference for users who want to follow up on the theoretical background or compare with alternative implementations.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#1.-Wave-Dark-Matter-Theory","page":"References and citations","text":"Hu, W., Barkana, R. & Gruzberg, A. (2000). Fuzzy cold dark matter: the wave properties of ultralight particles. Phys. Rev. Lett. 85, 1158. — hu2000fuzzy. The original proposal of fuzzy / wave CDM as a way to address small-scale CDM challenges.\nHui, L. (2021). Wave dark matter. Annu. Rev. Astron. Astrophys. 59, 247. — hui2021wavedark. A modern review of wave CDM: de Broglie scale, solitons, granules, observations.\nHui, L., Ostriker, J. P., Tremaine, S. & Witten, E. (2017). Ultralight scalars as cosmological dark matter. Phys. Rev. D 95, 043541. — hui2017ultralight. Defines the quantum pressure and \"quantum energy\" used in virial diagnostics.\nChavanis, P.-H. (2019). Predictive model of BEC dark matter halos with a solitonic core and a quantum pressure bulge. Phys. Rev. D 100, 083022. — chavanis2019predictive. Critical-temperature estimate for ultralight bosons.\nKagan, Yu., Muryshev, A. E. & Shlyapnikov, G. V. (1997). Evolution of correlation properties and appearance of interference in the course of Bose-Einstein condensation. Phys. Rev. Lett. 81, 933. — kagan1997evolutioncorrelation. Justifies the classical-field GPE in the highly-degenerate regime.\nDong, X.-B. et al. (2025). The promise of wave dark matter. (in prep.). — dong2025promise. Discussion of the excited-condensate component and its observational consequences (JWST).\nLiu, W.-J. et al. (2025). Warm fuzzy dark matter. (in prep.). — liu2025warmfuzzy. Wave warm dark matter, an alternative to the cold case.\nChiang, B. T., Ostriker, J. P. & Schive, H.-Y. (2021). Soliton oscillations and revised constraints from the soliton–halo relation and from pulsar timing. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 508, 3945. — chiang2021soliton. Ground-state oscillation period tau_00.","title":"1. Wave Dark Matter Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#2.-Schrödinger–Poisson-Equation","page":"References and citations","text":"Pitaevskii, L. P. & Stringari, S. (2016). Bose–Einstein Condensation and Superfluidity. Oxford University Press. — pitaevskii2016boseeinstein. Background on the GPE.\nLiao, S. et al. (2025). Deciphering the soliton–halo relation and the quantum pressure in fuzzy dark matter halos. (in prep.). — liao2025decipheringsolitonhalo. Discusses the velocity-dispersion QSS diagnostic and the \"effective thermal energy\" terminology.\nMocz, P. et al. (2017). Galaxy formation with BECDM — I. Turbulence and relaxation of idealized halos. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 471, 4559. — mocz2017galaxy. Defines the \"gradient energy\" K_rho notation used in the virial-diagnostic literature.","title":"2. Schrödinger–Poisson Equation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#3.-Numerical-Methods","page":"References and citations","text":"Edwards, F., Kendall, E., Hotchkiss, S. & Sherwin, R. (2018). PyUltraLight: a pseudo-spectral solver for ultralight dark matter dynamics. (ascl:1808.008). — edwards2018pyultralight. The original pseudo-spectral SPE solver in Python; WaveDM.jl follows the same dimensionless-unit convention.\nGlennon, N., Musoke, N., Chatterjee, A. & Nikoleyko, D. (2021). Modifying SPH for simulations of ultralight bosonic dark matter. Phys. Rev. D 105, 123540. — glennon2021modifying. Mocks the same dimensionless units.\nSchwabe, B., Gosenca, M., Behrendt, C., Easther, R. & McDonald, J. (2016). Simulations of ultralight axion-like dark matter. Phys. Rev. D 95, 083513. — schwabe2016simulations. CFL timestep criterion.\nMay, S. & Springel, V. (2021). Structure formation in large-volume cosmological simulations of fuzzy dark matter. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 506, 2603. — may2021structure. Moving-mesh SPE.\nMay, S., Springel, V. & White, S. D. M. (2023). The halo structure of fuzzy dark matter. (in prep.). — may2023halo. Velocity resolution criterion.\nLi, X., Hui, L. & Yavetz, T. (2019). Numerical challenge of the fuzzy dark matter model. Phys. Rev. D 103, 023508. — li2019numerical. Resolution criteria; de Broglie-wavelength requirement.\nSchive, H.-Y., Chiueh, T. & Broadhurst, T. (2014, 2026 update). Cosmic structure as the quantum interference of a coherent dark wave. Nature Physics 10, 496; updated review preprint 2026. — schive2014cosmicstructure, schive2026fuzzydark. The reference paper for the SPE + AMR approach and a comprehensive review of methodologies.\nJames, R. A. (1977). The solution of Poisson's equation for isolated source distributions. J. Comput. Phys. 25, 71. — james1977solutionpoissons. The four-step algorithm implemented in PhysicalFFT.jl for isolated potentials; the current WaveDM.jl driver uses an isolated tree solve for boundary = Vacuum().\nDutta Chowdhury, D., van den Bosch, F. C., Robles, V. H., van Dokkum, P., Schive, H.-Y., Chiueh, T. & Broadhurst, T. (2021). On the random motion of the soliton in fuzzy dark matter halos. Astrophys. J. 916, 27. — duttachowdhury2021random. Box-size and resolution discussion for isolated halos.","title":"3. Numerical Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#4.-Self-Interaction-and-Nonlinear-Schrödinger","page":"References and citations","text":"Levkov, D., Panin, A. & Tkachev, I. (2018). Gravitational Bose-Einstein condensation in the kinetic regime. Phys. Rev. Lett. 121, 151301. — levkov2018gravitational.\nNori, M. & Baldi, M. (2018). AX-GADGET: a new code for cosmological simulations of fuzzy dark matter and axion models. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 478, 3935. — nori2018axgadget.","title":"4. Self-Interaction & Nonlinear Schrödinger"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#5.-Hydrodynamic-/-Madelung-based-Methods","page":"References and citations","text":"Mocz, P. & Succi, S. (2015). Numerical solution of the nonlinear Schrödinger equation using smoothed-particle hydrodynamics. Phys. Rev. E 91, 053304. — mocz2015numerical.\nVeltmaat, J., Niemeyer, J. C. & Schwabe, B. (2016). Formation and structure of ultralight bosonic dark matter halos. Phys. Rev. D 94, 123523. — veltmaat2016cosmological.\nVeltmaat, J., Schwabe, B. & Niemeyer, J. C. (2018). Formation of ultralight bosonic dark matter halos through a real scalar field. Phys. Rev. D 98, 043509. — veltmaat2018formation.\nZhang, J., Liu, H. & Chu, M.-C. (2018). Axion-Gadget: a smoothed-particle-hydrodynamics code for ultralight dark matter. (preprint). — zhang2018ultralight.\nMocz, P. et al. (2017). Galaxy formation with BECDM — I. Turbulence and relaxation of idealized halos. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 471, 4559. — mocz2017galaxy.\nMocz, P. et al. (2020). Galaxy formation with BECDM — II. Cosmic filaments and halos. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 494, 2027. — mocz2020galaxy.\nMocz, P. et al. (2025). jaxion: a high-performance code for ultralight dark matter. (preprint). — mocz2025jaxion.\nSchwabe, B. & Niemeyer, J. C. (2020). Simulations of solitonic core plus fuzzy dark halo formation. Phys. Rev. Lett. 124, 201301. — schwabe2020simulating.\nSchwabe, B., Gosenca, M., Behrendt, C., Easther, R. & McDonald, J. (2022). Deep learning for freckles in BEC dark matter. Phys. Rev. D 105, 123502. — schwabe2022deep.\nLague, A., Schwabe, B., Clough, K. & Niemeyer, J. C. (2021). Evolving fuzzy dark matter perturbations. Phys. Rev. D 104, 083536. — lague2021evolving.\nHopkins, P. F. et al. (2019). Stable hierarchical solar system and ultra-light dark matter. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 488, 4168. — hopkins2019stable.\nMina, M., Mota, D. F. & Winther, H. A. (2020). SCALAR: an AMR code for fuzzy dark matter. (preprint). — mina2020scalar.\nNori, M., Mocz, P., Davé, R. & Baldi, M. (2024). Fuzzy-Gasoline: a multi-fluid SPH code for ultralight bosonic dark matter. (preprint). — nori2024fuzzy.","title":"5. Hydrodynamic / Madelung-based Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#6.-Related-Simulation-Codes","page":"References and citations","text":"Musoke, N., Hotchkiss, S. & Easther, R. (2024). UltraDark.jl: a Julia package for ultralight dark matter dynamics. J. Open Source Softw. — musoke2024ultradarkjl. Closest Julia-language SPE cousin.\nKunkel, S. et al. (2025). Hybrid CPU/GPU and MPI parallelization of the GAMER code. Astrophys. J. Suppl. Ser. — kunkel2025hybrid. The MPI/AMR SPE implementation in GAMER.\nHlozek, R. et al. (2015). A search for ultralight axions using the CMB. Phys. Rev. D 91, 103512. — hlozek2015search. AxionCAMB.\n\nThe full list with method, boundary, capabilities is reproduced in Table 1 of the accompanying paper (Meng & Dong 2026).","title":"6. Related Simulation Codes"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#7.-Observational-Data-and-Galaxy-Models","page":"References and citations","text":"Battaglia, G., Taibi, S., Thomas, G. F. & Fritz, T. K. (2022). Gaia early DR3 systemic motions of Local Group dwarf galaxies. Astron. Astrophys. 657, A89. — battaglia2022gaia. Source catalogue for AstroIC.load_data_MW_satellites().\nHayashi, K. et al. (2023). Dark matter halos of dwarf galaxies from cosmological simulations. (preprint). — hayashi2023dark. Crater II gNFW fit used in the case study.\nZhu, Y. et al. (2023). How baryonic processes affect the Milky Way mass model. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. — zhu2023how. Multi-component MW mass model (bulge, disk, gas, halo).","title":"7. Observational Data & Galaxy Models"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#8.-QSS-/-Quasi-Stationary-State-Theory","page":"References and citations","text":"Lynden-Bell, D. (1967). Statistical mechanics of violent relaxation in stellar systems. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 136, 101. — lynden-bell1967statisticalmechanics.\nBenetti, F. P. C. & Marcos, B. (2014). Non-equilibrium phase transitions in a 3D self-gravitating system. (preprint). — benetti2014nonequilibrium.\nKlessen, R. S. (2000). Gravitational turbulence. Habilitation thesis. — klessen2000gravitational.\nBerman, R. H. & Tajima, T. (2012). Turbulence in self-gravitating systems. (preprint). — berman2012turbulence.\nMoon, S. et al. (2024). Theory of soliton–halo co-evolution in fuzzy dark matter. (preprint). — moon2024theory.\nChandrasekhar, S. (1953). Problems of stability in gravitational systems. Rev. Mod. Phys. — chandrasekhar1953problems.\nWoo, T.-P. & Chiueh, T. (2009). High-resolution simulation on structure formation with extremely light bosonic dark matter. Astrophys. J. 697, 850. — woo2009highresolution.","title":"8. QSS / Quasi-Stationary State Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#9.-Software-References","page":"References and citations","text":"","title":"9. Software References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#9.1-Key-dependencies","page":"References and citations","text":"Bezanson, J., Edelman, A., Karpinski, S. & Shah, V. B. (2017). Julia: a fresh approach to numerical computing. SIAM Review 59, 65. — The Julia language.\nFrigo, M. & Johnson, S. G. (2005). The design and implementation of FFTW3. Proc. IEEE 93, 216. — FFTW.jl.\nBesard, T., Foket, C. & De Sutter, B. (2019). Effective extensible programming: unleashing Julia on GPUs. IEEE Trans. Parallel Distrib. Syst. — CUDA.jl.\nByrne, S., Wilcox, L. C. & Hamrud, M. (2021). MPI.jl: Julia bindings for the Message Passing Interface. (preprint). — MPI.jl. (Not currently used; planned for a future release.)\nDanisch, S. & Krumbiegel, J. (2021). Makie.jl: flexible high-performance data visualization for Julia. J. Open Source Softw. — danisch2021makiejlflexible. GLMakie.jl, CairoMakie.jl.\nDarema, F. et al. (1988). A single-program-multiple-data computational model for EPEX/FORTRAN. Parallel Comput. — darema1988singleprogrammultipledatacomputational. The SPMD paradigm underlying WaveDM.jl's Distributed.jl integration.\nSpringel, V. (2005). The cosmological simulation code GADGET-2. Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 364, 1105. — springel2005cosmological. Tree / SPH reference.\nSpringel, V., Yoshida, N. & White, S. D. M. (2001). GADGET: a simulation environment for cosmological and galactic dynamics. New Astron. 6, 79. — springel2001gadget.\nGropp, W., Lusk, E. & Skjellum, A. (1999). Using MPI: portable parallel programming with the message-passing interface. MIT Press. — gropp1999usingmpi.","title":"9.1 Key dependencies"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#9.2-JuliaAstroSim-Ecosystem","page":"References and citations","text":"PhysicalParticles.jl — vector algebra and N-body particle types.\nPhysicalFFT.jl — FFT primitives for physics.\nPhysicalFDM.jl — finite-difference operators.\nPhysicalMeshes.jl — mesh bookkeeping.\nPhysicalTrees.jl — tree gravity.\nAstroSimBase.jl — common types and units.\nAstroNbodySim.jl — N-body glue and parallel runtime.\nAstroIC.jl — initial condition generation.\nAstroIO.jl — snapshot I/O.\nAstroPlot.jl — analysis and visualization.\nGalacticDynamics.jl — density profiles and orbits.\n\nThe full ecosystem is at https://github.com/JuliaAstroSim.","title":"9.2 JuliaAstroSim Ecosystem"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#10.-WaveDM.jl-Citation","page":"References and citations","text":"If you use WaveDM.jl in your research, please cite it as:\n\nMeng, R. & Dong, X. 2026, WaveDM.jl: An Adaptable Simulation Framework for Dynamics of Baryonic and Wave Dark Matter on Galaxy Scales, Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics — journal article · arXiv:2606.25026 · DOI 10.1088/1674-4527/ae9042\n\n@article{meng2026wavedmjladaptablea,\n  title = {{{WaveDM}}.Jl: {{An Adaptable Simulation Framework}} for {{Dynamics}} of {{Baryonic}} and {{Wave Dark Matter}} on {{Galaxy Scales}}},\n  shorttitle = {{{WaveDM}}.Jl},\n  author = {Meng, Runyu and Dong, Xiaobo},\n  year = 2026,\n  journal = {Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics},\n  issn = {1674-4527},\n  doi = {10.1088/1674-4527/ae9042},\n  url = {http://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1674-4527/ae9042},\n  eprint = {2606.25026},\n  archiveprefix = {arXiv},\n  primaryclass = {astro-ph.GA},\n  urldate = {2026-07-26}\n}\n\nPublications built on this code base:\n\nMeng, R. & Dong, X. 2026, Wave Cold Dark Matter Can Reproduce the Radial Acceleration Relation in X-ray Elliptical Galaxies, Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics — journal article · arXiv:2610.18969 · DOI 10.1088/1674-4527/aeac08\n\n@article{meng2026wavecold,\n  title = {Wave {{Cold Dark Matter Can Reproduce}} the {{Radial Acceleration Relation}} in {{X}}-Ray {{Elliptical Galaxies}}},\n  author = {Meng, Runyu and Dong, Xiaobo},\n  year = 2026,\n  journal = {Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics},\n  issn = {1674-4527},\n  doi = {10.1088/1674-4527/aeac08},\n  url = {http://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1674-4527/aeac08},\n  eprint = {2610.18969},\n  archiveprefix = {arXiv},\n  primaryclass = {astro-ph.GA},\n  urldate = {2026-09-24}\n}","title":"10. WaveDM.jl Citation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#11.-Online-Resources","page":"References and citations","text":"WaveDM.jl repository — https://github.com/JuliaAstroSim/WaveDM.jl\nWaveDM.jl documentation — https://JuliaAstroSim.github.io/WaveDM.jl/dev\nJuliaAstroSim organization — https://github.com/JuliaAstroSim\nJulia language — https://julialang.org/","title":"11. Online Resources"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Simulation","page":"Simulations","text":"","title":"Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Main-Simulation-Functions","page":"Simulations","text":"","title":"Main Simulation Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#simulate_waveDM","page":"Simulations","text":"The high-level entry point. It builds the initial conditions (using AstroIC.jl), creates a SimulationGrid, calls SPE3D_waveDM to evolve the SPE, then handles visualization and post-processing. Most of the keyword arguments listed below can be supplied directly to simulate_waveDM; the low-level-only ones are marked in the tables.","title":"simulate_waveDM"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#SPE3D_waveDM","page":"Simulations","text":"The lower-level entry point. It expects you to provide the initial wavefunction IC and (optionally) the baryonic density/potential/forces yourself. Use simulate_waveDM unless you are integrating WaveDM.jl into a larger pipeline.\n\nnote: Note\nDefaults in the tables below refer to simulate_waveDM. The low-level SPE3D_waveDM has different defaults: Xmax = 5, Nx = 64, Tmax = 1, Nt = 32, absorb_coeff = 0, gpu = true, and IC = (x,y,z) -> exp(-x²-y²-z²). Additional low-level keywords are forwarded to SPE3D_waveDM; IC itself is not a high-level keyword (use IC_vel).","title":"SPE3D_waveDM"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Simulation-Workflow","page":"Simulations","text":"flowchart LR\n    A[Grid setup] --> B[Initial conditions]\n    B --> C[Baryon setup<br/>mesh / static / dynamic]\n    C --> D[IC potential]\n    D --> E[Visualization init]\n    E --> F[KDK main loop]\n    F -->|every StepsBetweenSnapshots| E\n    F --> G[Outputs<br/>CSV, JLD2, PNG, MP4]\n\nGrid setup — uniform Cartesian grid (Nx × Ny × Nz, side lengths 2*Xmax etc.).\nInitial conditions — the initial wavefunction (IC_vel at the high level, IC at the low level) and V (external potential) are evaluated on the grid.\nBaryon setup — depending on baryon_mode, baryons are placed on the mesh, sampled as static N-body particles, or co-evolved as dynamic N-body particles.\nIC potential — the gravitational potential Phi_rm total at t = 0 is computed (FFT for periodic; an isolated tree solve for vacuum in the current driver).\nVisualization init — observables for Realtime and the per-snapshot update are wired up.\nKDK main loop — apply_kick_step! → apply_drift_step! for Nt steps.\nOutputs — per-step and final files (CSV for properties, JLD2 for psi/Phi, PNG/MP4 for visualization).","title":"Simulation Workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Key-Parameters","page":"Simulations","text":"","title":"Key Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Grid-and-resolution","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\nXmax, Ymax, Zmax 2.0, Xmax, Xmax Half-side length of the box in dimensionless units (full side L = 2*Xmax); low-level default is 5\nNx, Ny, Nz 256, Nx, Nx Grid points per axis; powers of two are fastest; low-level default is 64\nTmax 0.1 Total simulation time in dimensionless units (or u\"Gyr\" if Unitful is loaded); low-level default is 1\nNt 5000 Number of time steps (overridden when autoset_timestep = true); low-level default is 32\nbulk_perturb, bulk_size true, 8 Pooling of the initial velocity field; the paper's Crater II runs use bulk_size = 4","title":"Grid and resolution"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Initial-conditions","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\nIC_vel nothing High-level initial wavefunction: nothing generates it from model; a String loads a JLD2 written by save_IC = true; an array is used directly\nIC Gaussian (xyz)mapsto e^-x^2-y^2-z^2 Low-level (SPE3D_waveDM) initial wavefunction — a function of (xyz) returning a complex number\nV (x,y,z,ψ)->0 External / self-interaction potential V(mathbf r t psi)\nκ 0 Self-interaction strength. 0 (free), > 0 (repulsive), < 0 (attractive)\nbaryon 0 Static baryon density (low-level; used only by baryon_mode = :mesh)\nmₐ 0.2×10⁻²² eV/c² Boson mass in physical units (drives the dimensionless scale). The paper's Crater II runs use mₐ = 50 * 1e-22 * 1.783e-36u\"kg\"\nΩₘ₀ 0.31 Present-day matter density parameter\nMW_disk_RC false For model = :MW: drive the velocity field from the MW rotation curve\nbaryon_fraction_limit 1 Upper bound on the baryon-to-total mass ratio used when generating model = :dwarf ICs","title":"Initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Baryons","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\nbaryon_mode :mesh One of :ignored, :mesh, :particles_static, :particles_dynamic. Presets with no baryonic model (e.g. :dwarf_UFDs, :dwarf_NFW, :dwarf_Zhao) only accept :ignored. The paper quotes :ignored as the default; the current code default is :mesh\nbaryon_potential nothing Pre-computed Phi_rm baryon; skip the Poisson solve\nbaryon_particles nothing StructArray of N-body particles (Star / Gas) for static or dynamic modes\nSofteningLength 0.1u\"kpc\" Gravitational softening length for the N-body force, of order the mean interparticle spacing d=(VN)^13. The paper quotes ~1 kpc; the current code uses 0.1 kpc\nGravitySolver Tree() Tree() (mathcal O(Nlog N), Gadget-2) or DirectSum() (mathcal O(N^2))\n\nbaryon_mode values:\n\n:ignored — no baryons. Use this explicitly for dark-matter-only halos and for presets that do not model baryons; the package default is :mesh.\n:mesh — baryons live on the same FFT grid; potential computed by the same FFT Poisson solver as the DM. Best for spatially extended baryons (e.g. dSph, ETG).\n:particles_static — baryons are sampled once as N-body particles and held fixed; their gravitational potential is added to Phi_rm total at every step.\n:particles_dynamic — baryons are co-evolved with the DM; their potential is re-computed at every step and the DM acceleration is interpolated back to the particles. Most expensive, most realistic.","title":"Baryons"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Tidal-field","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\nMW_tidal_field false Add a time-dependent MW tidal field to Phi_rm total\nLMC_tidal_field false Add LMC perturbation (requires MW_tidal_field = true)\nMW_tidal_interpolate true Use a pre-computed splined MW potential grid (MW_pot) for speed\nMW_pot nothing Path to a JLD2 file or a pre-built potential grid\nMW_pot_Xmax, MW_pot_Ymax, MW_pot_Zmax 100.0u\"kpc\" Half-side lengths of the MW potential interpolation box\nMW_pot_N 512 Resolution of the MW potential interpolation box\nexport_MW_pot false If true, save the MW tidal-field interpolation grid to a JLD2 file\ntidal_BG_sim nothing Pre-built MW background N-body Simulation (bypasses MW_pot)\ntidal_lookback_time 0.0u\"Gyr\" How far back in time the tidal field is applied\ndf_traj empty DataFrame Trajectory table (columns time, x, y, z) from the lookback step. Must be supplied when MW_tidal_field = true\ntidal_initial_pos, tidal_initial_vel Crater II defaults Initial Galactocentric position / velocity of the satellite\nparticles_LMC nothing StructArray of LMC particles (alternative to analytic Plummer)","title":"Tidal field"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Boundary-conditions","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\nboundary Periodic() Periodic() (FFT-based) or Vacuum() (isolated; see Algorithms §4.2)\nabsorb_coeff 10 Absorbing-layer coefficient alpha; 0 disables the absorber. The low-level default is 0","title":"Boundary conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Timestep","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\nautoset_timestep false Choose Delta t from the CFL criterion automatically\nautoset_timestep_ratio 0.9 Safety factor zeta_t for the auto-timestep","title":"Timestep"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Performance","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\ngpu false Use CUDA.jl for FFT and element-wise work. The low-level default is true\ndistributed_memory false Use Distributed.jl DArray for grid arrays. The distributed FFT Poisson solver is not implemented in the current release, so the full SSFM loop still requires a single process\npids workers() Worker process IDs for distributed runs\nKDK_flag true If true, use the second-order kick–drift–kick scheme","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Visualization","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\nRealtime true Open an interactive GLMakie window\nunicode_plot false Render a terminal heat-map of the density slice via UnicodePlots.jl\nunicode_heatmap_width 150 Width in characters of the terminal heat-map\nStepsBetweenSnapshots 5 Update the visualization and save frames every N steps\nsize (2400, 1400) Figure size of the overview plot\nplotOptical false Plot baryonic (optical) properties in addition to wave-DM\nplotWaveDM false Plot the wave-DM-only view alongside the total\nplot_virial false Add a virial-energy axis to the live figure\ndynamic_colorrange true Auto-scale the colormap to the running maximum density\nextract_dwarf_granule false Post-process: extract the snapshot best fitting a target profile/RC\nextract_mode :profile :profile or :RC extraction mode\nextract_min_t 0.2u\"Gyr\" Earliest simulation time considered for extraction\nbest_fit_halo_mass false If true, optimize the halo mass during the RC fit","title":"Visualization"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Output","page":"Simulations","text":"Keyword Default Description\ntitle \"WaveDM_MOND_MW\" Run title (used in output filenames); the low-level default is \"WaveDM\"\noutputdir joinpath(@__DIR__, \"output/MOND\") Where CSV / JLD2 / PNG / MP4 files are written; the low-level default is joinpath(@__DIR__, \"output\")\nsave_IC true Save the initial conditions to disk\nsave_phi true Save the final Phi and psi to JLD2\nexport_particles false If true, return the sampled baryon particles (no SPE run)\nIC_only false If true, return the initial wavefunction (no SPE run)","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Minimal-Working-Examples","page":"Simulations","text":"","title":"Minimal Working Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#1.-Smoke-test-(CPU)","page":"Simulations","text":"using WaveDM\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model = :MW,\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,\n    Xmax = 2.0,\n    Tmax = 0.001,\n    Nt   = 3,\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 64,\n    gpu = false,\n    Realtime = false,\n    unicode_plot = true,\n    outputdir = mktempdir(),\n)","title":"1. Smoke test (CPU)"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#2.-GPU-production-run","page":"Simulations","text":"using WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model = :MW,\n    Xmax = 5.0,\n    Tmax = 5.0u\"Gyr\",\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 256,\n    Nt = 5000,\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    gpu = true,\n    Realtime = true,\n    plot_virial = true,\n    title = \"MW_256_T5Gyr\",\n    outputdir = \"results/MW_256_T5Gyr\",\n)","title":"2. GPU production run"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#3.-Crater-II-ultra-faint-dwarf","page":"Simulations","text":"using WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model       = :dwarf_UFDs,\n    Galaxy_id   = 6,                 # Crater II\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,          # Crater II is DM-dominated\n    mₐ          = 50 * 1e-22 * 1.783e-36u\"kg\",  # m₂₂ = 50\n    V           = (x, y, z, ψ) -> 0.0,\n    Nx          = 384,\n    Xmax        = 20u\"kpc\",\n    Tmax        = 6.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    absorb_coeff = 10,\n    bulk_size   = 4,                 # value used in the paper\n    gpu         = true,\n    Realtime    = true,\n    title       = \"CraterII\",\n)","title":"3. Crater II ultra-faint dwarf"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#4.-Crater-II-with-MW-tidal-field","page":"Simulations","text":"using WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model       = :dwarf_UFDs,\n    Galaxy_id   = 6,\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,\n    mₐ          = 50 * 1e-22 * 1.783e-36u\"kg\",\n    V           = (x, y, z, ψ) -> 0.0,\n    Nx          = 384,\n    Xmax        = 20u\"kpc\",\n    Tmax        = 6.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    absorb_coeff = 10,\n    MW_tidal_field = true,\n    df_traj     = df_traj,          # from the lookback step (see Examples §4)\n    tidal_lookback_time = 6.0u\"Gyr\",\n    LMC_tidal_field = true,         # include LMC perturbation\n    gpu         = true,\n    title       = \"CraterII_MW_LMC\",\n)","title":"4. Crater II with MW tidal field"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#5.-Baryons-as-dynamic-N-body-particles","page":"Simulations","text":"using WaveDM\nusing AstroIC\nusing PhysicalParticles, Unitful\n\nbaryons = generate(AstroIC.ExponentialDisk(; ...))  # see AstroIC.jl\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    Nx = 256,\n    Xmax = 5.0,\n    Tmax = 2.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    baryon_mode       = :particles_dynamic,\n    baryon_particles  = baryons,\n    GravitySolver     = Tree(),\n    SofteningLength   = 0.1u\"kpc\",           # code default\n    gpu = true,\n)","title":"5. Baryons as dynamic N-body particles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#6.-Distributed-run","page":"Simulations","text":"warning: Warning\nThe distributed FFT Poisson solver is not implemented in the current release: parallel_poisson raises an error for kind = :distributed. Distributed workers currently accelerate initial-condition generation and N-body work, but a full SPE run must use the serial/threads or GPU backend.\n\nusing Distributed\naddprocs(8)\n@everywhere using WaveDM\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    Nx = Ny = Nz = 256,\n    Tmax = 2.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    distributed_memory = true,         # use DArray for grid arrays\n    gpu = false,                       # GPU and distributed are not orthogonal but mixing\n                                      # them is an advanced use case\n)","title":"6. Distributed run"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Return-Values","page":"Simulations","text":"ψ, fig, figMOND, chi2RC, dfProp, dfAcc, averaged_ψ2 = simulate_waveDM(...)\n\nReturn Description\nψ Final wavefunction (complex array, dimensionless units)\nfig Overview Makie figure (snapshot of the live plot at end of run)\nfigMOND MOND / RAR diagnostic figure, or nothing if baryon_mode = :ignored\nchi2RC chi^2 of the rotation-curve fit, or nothing for DM-only runs\ndfProp DataFrame with the time series of total mass, mass-fraction radii, virial energies, momenta\ndfAcc DataFrame with the radial acceleration profile (DM-only or DM+baryon+MOND)\naveraged_ψ2 Time-averaged $","title":"Return Values"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Output-Files","page":"Simulations","text":"The simulation writes several file types into outputdir:\n\n$(title), $(suffix) - Overview IC.png — initial snapshot\n$(title), $(suffix) - Overview.mp4 — animation of the entire run (if Realtime = true)\n$(title), $(suffix) - Overview Prop.png — final-state overview\n$(title), $(suffix) - RC.png / RAR.png — rotation curve / radial acceleration relation\n$(title), $(suffix) - Prop best fit.jld2 — best-fit snapshot (if extract_dwarf_granule = true)\n$(title), $(suffix) - acc.csv / acc best fit.csv — radial acceleration data\n$(title), $(suffix) - best_fit_time.csv — best-fit simulation time\n$(title), $(suffix) - timestep_*.jld2 — per-snapshot ψ / Φ (if average_snapshots = true)\n\nThe suffix is a human-readable summary of the run, e.g. Nx(384), Xmax(20.00), Nt(377), Tmax(6.00), DM_m(1.00).","title":"Output Files"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Performance-Considerations","page":"Simulations","text":"GPU vs CPU. gpu = true is most useful for grids gtrsim 256^3. For tiny problems the kernel-launch overhead dominates and CPU is faster. On a 12 GB device, N gtrsim 512 becomes memory-limited.\nDistributed memory. The distributed FFT Poisson solver is not implemented yet; use distributed workers for IC generation and N-body work only.\nGrid size. Powers of two (64, 128, 256, …) maximize FFT performance. The code also runs on non-power-of-two grids.\nTimestep. autoset_timestep = true keeps Delta t near the CFL limit without manual tuning.\nStepsBetweenSnapshots. Larger values skip more visualization updates per step, which is useful when the plot is the bottleneck.\nMemory. On GPU, N^3 complex arrays dominate. A 512^3 single-precision grid is sim 2 GiB; a 1024^3 grid is sim 16 GiB.\nKDK_flag. Leave at true. Setting it to false reduces the scheme to first-order Lie splitting and is provided only for code-experimentation purposes.","title":"Performance Considerations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/simulation/#Related-Modules","page":"Simulations","text":"Configs — configuration structures\nKDK — the kick–drift–kick implementation","title":"Related Modules"},{"category":"function","location":"api/simulation/#WaveDM.simulate_waveDM","page":"Simulations","text":"Test function for Milky Way-like simulations with MOND.\n\nThis function sets up and runs a simulation of the Milky Way or dwarf galaxies with MOND effects.\n\nArguments\n\nmodel: Simulation model type (:MW for Milky Way, :dwarf for dwarf galaxies)\nV: External potential function\nFDM_mass_ratio: Ratio of FDM mass to total mass\nFDM_radius_ratio: Ratio of FDM radius to total radius\ntitle: Simulation title for output files\nXmax, Ymax, Zmax: Simulation box dimensions (in dimensionless units)\nTmax: Total simulation time (in dimensionless units)\nNt: Number of time steps\nNx, Ny, Nz: Number of grid points in each dimension\nNp: Number of particles for each baryon component\nabsorb_coeff: Absorption coefficient at boundaries\nStepsBetweenSnapshots: Frequency of visualization updates\nIC_vel: Initial velocity field (or path to load from file)\nIC_only: If true, return initial conditions without running simulation\nstatic: If true, no velocity, phases are all zero\noutputdir: Directory for saving output files\nmassRadius: Mass radius for the galaxy\nbaryon_β, baryon_ρ0, baryon_r0: Baryonic density profile parameters\nhalo_β, halo_ρ0, halo_r0, halo_α, halo_γ, halo_Q: Dark halo density profile parameters\nGravitySolver: Gravitational solver type (Tree() or DirectSummation())\nboundary: Boundary conditions (Periodic() or Vacuum())\nSofteningLength: Gravitational softening length\nbaryon_mode: How baryons are treated (:mesh, :particlesstatic, :particlesdynamic, :ignored)\ngpu: Enable GPU acceleration\n\nReturns\n\nIf IC_only=true: Initial velocity field IC_vel\nOtherwise: Results from SPE3D_waveDM function\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.simulate_waveDM"},{"category":"function","location":"api/simulation/#WaveDM.SPE3D_waveDM","page":"Simulations","text":"Main simulation function for 3D Schrödinger-Poisson equation with MOND.\n\nThis function handles the complete simulation workflow from initial condition setup to result visualization.\n\nArguments\n\nXmax, Ymax, Zmax: Simulation box dimensions (in dimensionless units)\nTmax: Total simulation time (in dimensionless units)\nNx, Ny, Nz: Number of grid points in each dimension\nNt: Number of time steps\nautoset_timestep: Automatically set timestep based on stability criteria\nIC: Initial wavefunction or distribution function\nV: External potential function\nκ: Self-interaction parameter\nbaryon: Baryonic density contribution\nbaryon_mode: How baryons are treated (:ignored, :mesh, :particlesstatic, :particlesdynamic)\nbaryon_potential: Pre-computed potentials of static baryons\nax_b, ay_b, az_b: Baryonic acceleration components\nabsorb_coeff: Absorption coefficient at boundaries\noutputdir: Directory for saving output files\ntitle: Simulation title for output files\nboundary: Boundary conditions (Periodic() or Vacuum())\nSofteningLength: Gravitational softening length\ngpu: Enable GPU acceleration\nRealtime: Enable real-time visualization\nStepsBetweenSnapshots: Frequency of visualization updates\nFDM_mass_ratio: Ratio of FDM mass to total mass\nFDM_radius_ratio: Ratio of FDM radius to total radius\n\nReturns\n\nψ: Final wavefunction\nfig: Overview plot figure\nfigMOND: MOND-related plot figure\nchi2RC: Chi-squared value for rotation curve fit\ndfProp: DataFrame with simulation properties\ndfAcc: DataFrame with acceleration data\naveraged_ψ2: Time-averaged wavefunction squared\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"WaveDM.SPE3D_waveDM"},{"category":"section","location":"#WaveDM.jl","page":"Home","text":"WaveDM.jl: An Adaptable Simulation Framework for Dynamics of Baryonic and Wave Dark Matter on Galaxy Scales\n\nWaveDM.jl is an open-source Julia package for high-performance simulations of wave (fuzzy) dark matter dynamics on galaxy scales. The code solves the time-dependent Schrödinger–Poisson equation (SPE) using a pseudo-spectral split-step Fourier method, with a design philosophy centered on adaptability and extensibility.\n\nThe spectral solver is tightly integrated with N-body gravitational force solvers, enabling simultaneous evolution of wave dark matter and baryonic components in galaxy-scale simulations. A unified multi-level parallelization framework brings shared-memory, distributed-memory, and GPU execution into a single workflow. The same code runs across CPU, multi-threaded, and GPU configurations; distributed workers currently accelerate initial-condition generation and N-body work, while the full SPE loop runs on a single process in this release. A dedicated galaxy-simulation toolbox — built on AstroIC.jl, AstroNbodySim.jl, and AstroPlot.jl — bundles flexible initial condition generators, trajectory lookback, tidal force calculations, and real-time visualization.\n\nBeyond astrophysics, the code's modular architecture supports cross-disciplinary studies in nonlinear optics and cold-atom physics through a generalized nonlinear Schrödinger interface.","title":"WaveDM.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Three-Key-Capabilities","page":"Home","text":"Coupled wave + N-body evolution. The SPE solver evolves the bosonic field psi on a uniform grid, while AstroNbodySim.jl simultaneously evolves baryonic particles (static or fully dynamic) and computes their gravitational coupling to the wave dark matter. Time-dependent tidal fields from a host halo and external perturbers (e.g. the LMC) are supported.\nMulti-level parallel execution. Shared-memory multi-threading, distributed-memory parallelism via Julia's native Distributed.jl, and GPU acceleration via CUDA.jl are all exposed through simple keyword arguments (-t N, distributed_memory=true, gpu=true). The same script runs unchanged from a laptop to a multi-node cluster.\nGalaxy-simulation toolbox. Ready-to-use density profiles (gNFW, NFW, Zhao), Milky-Way and SPARC initial conditions, Milky-Way satellite trajectory lookback (Battaglia 2022), MW / LMC tidal fields, on-the-fly virial and energy diagnostics, and interactive 2D/3D visualization via GLMakie.jl (and terminal-based UnicodePlots.jl for headless runs).","title":"Three Key Capabilities"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"The minimum useful script — wave CDM evolution of a Crater II-like ultra-faint dwarf:\n\nusing WaveDM\nusing Unitful\n\nsimulate_waveDM(;\n    model       = :dwarf_UFDs,\n    Galaxy_id   = 6,                # Crater II\n    baryon_mode = :ignored,         # Crater II is dark-matter dominated\n    mₐ          = 50 * 1e-22 * 1.783e-36u\"kg\",  # m₂₂ = 50\n    V           = (x, y, z, ψ) -> 0.0,  # external potential\n    Nx          = 384,              # mesh size\n    Xmax        = 20u\"kpc\",         # half-side length, L = 2*Xmax\n    Tmax        = 6.0u\"Gyr\",\n    autoset_timestep = true,\n    bulk_size   = 4,                # value used in the paper\n    gpu         = true,\n    Realtime    = true,\n)\n\nSee Examples for additional end-to-end cases (Crater II with the MW tidal field, MW+LMC trajectory lookback, distributed-memory IC generation, GPU performance).","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Architecture","page":"Home","text":"(Image: WaveDM.jl architecture (taken from the accompanying paper))\n\nFigure 1 — Architecture of WaveDM.jl. The framework integrates four core components (reproduced from the accompanying paper, Meng & Dong 2026):\n\na pseudo-spectral split-step Fourier method (SSFM) solver for the SPE, evolving psi with a second-order kick–drift–kick (KDK) scheme;\nan optional baryonic physics module (AstroNbodySim.jl) with mesh-based and particle-based treatments (baryon_mode = :ignored | :mesh | :particles_static | :particles_dynamic), supporting both DirectSum (mathcalO(N^2)) and Tree (mathcalO(N log N), Gadget-2 scheme) gravity solvers;\na galaxy-simulation toolbox providing trajectory lookback, tidal-field evaluation, logging, real-time visualization (AstroPlot.jl), and post-processing;\na multi-level parallel framework combining multi-threading, distributed-memory (Julia's Distributed.jl with TCP/IP transport), and GPU acceleration (CUDA.jl).\n\nInitial conditions are generated by AstroIC.jl; snapshot I/O is handled by AstroIO.jl. Gas dynamics via SPH is planned for a future release. MPI support is also planned as an alternative communication backend to Distributed.jl.","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"#Observational-constraints-on-the-ultralight-dark-matter-mass","page":"Home","text":"WaveDM.jl is designed for halo-scale simulations of fuzzy / wave dark matter, and a substantial literature already constrains the particle mass m_a across more than nine orders of magnitude. The table below summarises 42 observational and theoretical bounds from the literature, grouped by data type.\n\n(Image: Axion mass constraints)\n\nStatic PNG (click to enlarge). The rows follow the order of ma_constraints.tex and are split into Include bounds — allowed / favoured regions (green) and Exclude bounds — ruled-out regions (orange, hatched); within each group the shade of the bar encodes the dataset family (rotation curves, dwarf galaxies, Lyα forest, 21 cm, CMB, lensing, black holes, …) and a dashed guide marks every row. Every paper occupies exactly one row: works that quote two intervals (Bar et al. 2019, Marsh et al. 2019, Wardana et al. 2026 …) draw two bars on that row, and two *different papers that share an author–year label get a / b suffixes. The data — method annotation of each bar uses standard abbreviations (RC = rotation curve, SH relation = soliton–halo relation, SHMF = subhalo mass function, Lyα P(k) = Lyα flux power spectrum); an arrowhead marks a bound that continues past the plotted range, and a hatched cap marks a bound whose quoted value is itself a range (Marsh & Niemeyer 2019). For a copy-able vector version use ma_constraints.pdf.*\n\nFor the interactive version — hover any bar to see the citekey, year, title, authors, journal, DOI/arXiv link, raw constraint, and the bounding interval in log_10(m_amathrmeV) — open the plotly figure:\n\n<iframe src=\"../assets/ma_constraints.html\" width=\"100%\" height=\"980\" frameborder=\"0\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"border:1px solid #ddd\"></iframe>\n\nA lightweight fallback (pure SVG, no JavaScript) is also available; click any rectangle in ma_constraints.svg to open the paper DOI in a new tab.\n\nThe raw constraint table is in docs/constraints/ma_constraints.tex; the structured dataset is in docs/constraints/output/ma_constraints.yaml; see docs/constraints/README.md for how the figure is regenerated.","title":"Observational constraints on the ultralight dark-matter mass"},{"category":"section","location":"#When-to-use-WaveDM.jl","page":"Home","text":"If you need … WaveDM.jl provides …\nGalaxy-scale SPE simulations with realistic halo potentials model = :MW, :dwarf, :dwarf_NFW, :dwarf_Zhao, :Elliptical, :cluster_NFW, :cluster_Burkert; gNFW / NFW / Zhao / β-model / Jaffe profiles; the Milky-Way mass model (Zhu 2023); Crater II preset (model = :dwarf_UFDs, Galaxy_id = 6; for profile fitting set target_profile_model = :dwarf_Zhao and pass the Crater II target_profile_* parameters from AstroIC.load_data_UFDs())\nTidal stripping of a satellite under a live host MW_tidal_field=true, optional LMC_tidal_field=true\nBackward orbital integration of a satellite AstroIC.load_data_MW_satellites() + an AstroNbodySim.Simulation\nSelf-interacting wave dark matter (kappa neq 0) Pluggable V(x, y, z, ψ) + κ keyword\nCross-disciplinary nonlinear Schrödinger problems Pluggable potential interface — same code base for NLO, BEC","title":"When to use WaveDM.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation-Navigation","page":"Home","text":"Quickstart — for first-time users; choose a model, run Crater II in under 10 minutes\nIntroduction — wave dark matter background, design philosophy, ecosystem, model decision tree\nInstallation — installing WaveDM.jl and its dependencies\nAlgorithms — SPE, split-step Fourier method, boundary conditions, resolution and timestep criteria, virial diagnostics\nAPI Reference — detailed API documentation\nExamples — complete simulation examples\nReferences — related literature","title":"Documentation Navigation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Home","text":"If you use WaveDM.jl in your research, please cite the accompanying paper:\n\nMeng, R. & Dong, X. 2026, WaveDM.jl: An Adaptable Simulation Framework for Dynamics of Baryonic and Wave Dark Matter on Galaxy Scales, Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics — journal article · arXiv:2606.25026 · DOI 10.1088/1674-4527/ae9042\n\n@article{meng2026wavedmjladaptablea,\n  title = {{{WaveDM}}.Jl: {{An Adaptable Simulation Framework}} for {{Dynamics}} of {{Baryonic}} and {{Wave Dark Matter}} on {{Galaxy Scales}}},\n  shorttitle = {{{WaveDM}}.Jl},\n  author = {Meng, Runyu and Dong, Xiaobo},\n  year = 2026,\n  journal = {Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics},\n  issn = {1674-4527},\n  doi = {10.1088/1674-4527/ae9042},\n  url = {http://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1674-4527/ae9042},\n  eprint = {2606.25026},\n  archiveprefix = {arXiv},\n  primaryclass = {astro-ph.GA},\n  urldate = {2026-07-26}\n}\n\nPublications built on this code base:\n\nMeng, R. & Dong, X. 2026, Wave Cold Dark Matter Can Reproduce the Radial Acceleration Relation in X-ray Elliptical Galaxies, Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics — journal article · arXiv:2610.18969 · DOI 10.1088/1674-4527/aeac08\n\n@article{meng2026wavecold,\n  title = {Wave {{Cold Dark Matter Can Reproduce}} the {{Radial Acceleration Relation}} in {{X}}-Ray {{Elliptical Galaxies}}},\n  author = {Meng, Runyu and Dong, Xiaobo},\n  year = 2026,\n  journal = {Research in Astronomy and Astrophysics},\n  issn = {1674-4527},\n  doi = {10.1088/1674-4527/aeac08},\n  url = {http://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1674-4527/aeac08},\n  eprint = {2610.18969},\n  archiveprefix = {arXiv},\n  primaryclass = {astro-ph.GA},\n  urldate = {2026-09-24}\n}","title":"Citation"},{"category":"section","location":"#License","page":"Home","text":"WaveDM.jl is released under the GNU General Public License v3.0 (GPL-3). See the LICENSE file for details.","title":"License"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#Algorithms","page":"Algorithms","text":"This page describes the numerical methods used by WaveDM.jl. It mirrors the structure of §§2–3 of the accompanying paper (Meng & Dong 2026) and uses the same sign conventions. All equations are written in the dimensionless units introduced in Introduction §1.3.","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#1.-The-Schrödinger–Poisson-System","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"1. The Schrödinger–Poisson System"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#1.1-Dimensionless-form","page":"Algorithms","text":"beginaligned\nipartial_t psi(mathbf r t) = -tfrac12nabla^2psi + Phi_rm total(mathbf r t)psi \nnabla^2 Phi_rm total(mathbf r t) = 4pibigl(rho_rm DM + rho_rm baryonbigr)\nendaligned\n\nwith rho_rm DM = psi^2 and Phi_rm total = Phi_rm DM + Phi_rm baryon. The wavefunction is written in polar form as psi = sqrtrhoe^itheta, with velocity field mathbf v = nablatheta.","title":"1.1 Dimensionless form"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#1.2-Generalized-form","page":"Algorithms","text":"For non-astrophysical applications WaveDM.jl solves the generalized nonlinear Schrödinger equation (GNLSE):\n\nipartial_t psi(mathbf r t) = -tfrac12nabla^2psi + V(mathbf r t psi)psi\n\nwhere V is an arbitrary, user-supplied potential. The two native classes are:\n\nLocal — V = F(psi(mathbf r t)), depending only on the field amplitude at the same point. Example: Kerr nonlinearity V = kappapsi^2 used for self-interacting wave CDM and instantaneous nonlinear optical response.\nNonlocal — V is an integral over the field distribution, V = int U(mathbf r - mathbf r)psi(mathbf r)^2 d^3 r, or equivalently the solution of an auxiliary field equation (e.g. Poisson for self-gravity, dipole–dipole in atomic condensates, thermal-optical nonlinearities in NLO).","title":"1.2 Generalized form"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#1.3-Madelung-form-and-the-quantum-energy","page":"Algorithms","text":"The Madelung transformation yields the continuity equation and the quantum Bernoulli equation. The quantum potential\n\nQ = -frachbar^22mfracnabla^2sqrtrhosqrtrho\n   = -frachbar^24mleftfracnabla^2rhorho - frac12frac(nablarho)^2rho^2right\n\nencodes the Heisenberg uncertainty principle. Its density-weighted volume integral is the quantum (gradient) energy mathcal Q:\n\nmathcal Q equiv int rho Qd^3 r\n             = frachbar^22mint nablasqrtrho^2 d^3r\n\nwhere the surface term vanishes for any realistic NFW-like halo (power-law decay rho propto r^-n with n  1). In dimensionless units mathcal Q = int nablasqrtrho^2 d^3r.\n\nThe quantum pressure is -(1m)nabla Q and appears in the quantum Euler equation. Three synonyms appear in the literature: quantum energy (Hui et al. 2017), gradient energy K_rho (Mocz et al. 2017), and effective thermal energy (Liao et al. 2025) — they are all the same density-weighted volume integral of Q.\n\nThe total energy of an isolated, fully self-gravitating halo is\n\nmathcal E = mathcal K + mathcal V + mathcal Q\n\nwhere mathcal K is the volume-integrated kinetic energy and mathcal V the gravitational potential energy. In simulations with fixed baryonic backgrounds and absorbing boundaries mathcal E is not strictly conserved (see Section 5.1 below).\n\nFor a wave-CDM halo governed by the SPE, the virial theorem at virial equilibrium takes the form\n\n2mathcal K + 2mathcal Q + mathcal V = 0\n\nwhich can be evaluated from a single simulation snapshot (or, equivalently, from single-epoch observational data of a realistic galaxy). It is the cheapest global test of equilibrium, but it is only a necessary condition: a halo can satisfy the virial relation while its radial mass distribution and velocity field are still evolving (see Section 6.3 for the more stringent, spatially resolved test).","title":"1.3 Madelung form and the quantum energy"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#2.-The-Split-Step-Fourier-Method","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"2. The Split-Step Fourier Method"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#2.1-Operator-splitting","page":"Algorithms","text":"Write the evolution operator as the sum of two pieces:\n\npartial_t psi = bigl(hat D + hat Nbigr)psi\n\nwith the kinetic dispersion operator hat D = tfraci2nabla^2 and the potential operator hat N = -iPhi_rm total. Both pieces are exactly solvable (linear in their respective representation). The Strang second-order symmetric split is\n\npsi(mathbf r t+epsilon) approx e^fracepsilon 2 hat Ne^epsilon hat De^fracepsilon 2 hat Npsi(mathbf r t)\n\nThis is the \"kick–drift–kick\" (KDK) time-integration scheme. For a real potential and no absorbing layer, both split operators are unitary, so the total mass is conserved to machine precision. In Fourier space the drift is diagonal:\n\ne^epsilon hat Dlongrightarrowe^-fraci2epsilonk^2","title":"2.1 Operator splitting"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#2.2-The-four-computational-steps","page":"Algorithms","text":"Let mathcal F denote the forward FFT and mathcal F^-1 its inverse. With the time-averaging potential update between the two kicks, the KDK step becomes:\n\nbeginaligned\ntext(a) psi_1 leftarrow e^fracepsilon 2hat Npsi(mathbf r t) \ntext(b) tildepsi leftarrow mathcal Fpsi_1quad\n                        tildepsi leftarrow e^-fraci2epsilon k^2tildepsiquad\n                        psi_2 leftarrow mathcal F^-1tildepsi \ntext(c) rho leftarrow psi_2^2 \n          Phi_rm DM leftarrow mathcal F^-1left-frac4pik^2mathcal Fbiglrho - langlerhoranglebigrright \n          Phi_rm total leftarrow Phi_rm DM + Phi_rm baryon \ntext(d) psi(mathbf r t+epsilon) leftarrow e^fracepsilon 2hat Npsi_2 \n          textabsorbing boundary quad psi leftarrow psi odot mathcal B(mathbf r) \n          textsnapshot if n bmod n_rm stride = 0\nendaligned\n\nStep (c) — the gravity update — is what makes the scheme a self-consistent SPE solver: the kinetic drift uses the old potential, but the second kick uses an updated potential that incorporates the new density field. This averaging is essential for second-order accuracy. In the implementation, step (c) is evaluated at the start of the next kick; because the half kick is a pure phase factor, psi^2 is unchanged by it, so this is equivalent to evaluating the potential immediately after the drift.","title":"2.2 The four computational steps"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#2.3-Algorithm-1-—-split-step-Fourier-loop","page":"Algorithms","text":"The full main loop, in pseudocode, is:\n\ninput : ψ(r, 0), Δt, T_max\noutput: ψ(r, T_max)\nn_max      ← ⌊T_max / Δt⌋\nn_stride   ← ⌊T_snap / Δt⌋\n\n# initial potentials\nΦ_DM       ← F⁻¹{ −4π/k² · F{ |ψ(r,0)|² − ⟨|ψ(r,0)|²⟩ } }\nΦ_total    ← Φ_DM + Φ_baryon\n\nfor n = 0, 1, …, n_max − 1 do\n    # Step (a): first half potential kick\n    ψ ← exp(Δt/2 · N̂) ψ        with N̂ = −i Φ_total\n\n    # Step (b): kinetic drift in Fourier space\n    ψ̃ ← F{ψ}                   # forward FFT\n    ψ̃ ← exp(−i/2 · Δt · k²) · ψ̃\n    ψ ← F⁻¹{ψ̃}                 # inverse FFT\n\n    # Step (c): update gravitational potential\n    ρ  ← |ψ|²\n    Φ_DM     ← F⁻¹{ −4π/k² · F{ρ − ⟨ρ⟩} }\n    Φ_total  ← Φ_DM + Φ_baryon\n\n    # Step (d): second half potential kick\n    ψ ← exp(Δt/2 · N̂) ψ\n\n    # Absorbing boundary condition\n    ψ ← ψ ⊙ B(r)\n\n    if n mod n_stride = 0 then save ψ(r, t = n·Δt)\nend\nreturn ψ(r, T_max)\n\nIn code the steps (a), (b), (c), (d) map to apply_kick_step! and apply_drift_step! in the KDK module — see KDK for signatures.","title":"2.3 Algorithm 1 — split-step Fourier loop"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#2.4-FFT-complexity","page":"Algorithms","text":"mathcal F\n\nand mathcal F^-1 use FFTW.jl (CPU) or CUDA.jl (GPU). For an N^3 grid a transform costs mathcal O(N^3log N), so an entire KDK step has the same complexity.","title":"2.4 FFT complexity"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#3.-Spatial-Discretization","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"3. Spatial Discretization"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#3.1-Uniform-Cartesian-grid","page":"Algorithms","text":"The simulation uses a uniform Cartesian grid with N_xtimes N_ytimes N_z points (keywords Nx, Ny, Nz). For nearly spherical halos a cubic grid N^3 is conventional. The cell sizes are\n\nDelta x = fracL_xN_x-1 quad\nDelta y = fracL_yN_y-1 quad\nDelta z = fracL_zN_z-1\n\nand grid coordinates are\n\nx_i = -tfracL2 + iDelta x i = 0dotsN_x-1\n\nwith the same form for y_j, z_k (keywords Xmax, Ymax, Zmax set the half-side lengths; the full side length is L = 2X_max). The time evolution uses the exact Fourier multiplier e^-ik^2Delta t2; finite differences with a second-order central stencil are used only for post-processing gradients (e.g. the density gradient).","title":"3.1 Uniform Cartesian grid"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#3.2-Resolution-criteria","page":"Algorithms","text":"Five criteria guide the choice of grid resolution. The code prioritises criterion (1) and dynamically adjusts L_x accordingly.\n\n# Criterion Reason\n1 Delta x lesssim lambda_rm dB3 Resolves quantum oscillations and avoids phase aliasing\n2 Delta x lesssim r_s3 Adequately samples the density field around the scale radius\n3 Delta x lesssim 03 kpc Accurate inner-slope fitting for dwarf halos\n4 L_x gtrsim 10lambda_rm dB Suppresses boundary artifacts in the soliton core\n5 L_x gtrsim 8r_s Adequate gravitational potential resolution\n\nFor m_22 equiv m_a  10^-22rm eV = 10 and v_rm rotsim 130 km s^-1 one obtains lambda_rm dB sim 077 kpc, giving the practical recommendations Delta x le 04 kpc and N ge 256 for L = 80 kpc. Empirically, Delta x lesssim lambda_rm dB3 is needed for unbiased velocity fields, requiring N ge 512 at m_22 = 10. Grid dimensions should be powers of two for FFT efficiency.\n\nThe maximum resolvable velocity is v_max = (hbarm)(piDelta x); the conservative choice v_max ge 2maxv_rm rot is required for unbiased dynamics.","title":"3.2 Resolution criteria"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#4.-Boundary-Conditions","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"4. Boundary Conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#4.1-Periodic-(default)","page":"Algorithms","text":"boundary = Periodic() is the natural choice for the Fourier-based solver. The Poisson equation is solved in Fourier space by\n\nPhi_rm DM(mathbf k) = -frac4pik^2biglrho(mathbf k) - langlerhorangledelta(mathbf k)bigr\n\nwhere langlerhorangle is the spatial average of the density — subtracting it removes the mathbf k = 0 singularity of the Laplacian.\n\nTo approximate isolated (vacuum) boundary conditions on a periodic domain, the box size should be at least eight times the halo scale radius, ensuring that no substantial bulk mass crosses the boundaries. This is the recommended choice for production runs.\n\nThe JuliaAstroSim stack also provides a finite-difference Poisson solver (PhysicalFDM.jl), used for 1D and 2D verification problems. It requires a dense or sparse difference matrix of size mathcal O(N^2d) and is not used for the 3D production runs, where the FFT solver is preferred.","title":"4.1 Periodic (default)"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#4.2-Vacuum-(isolated)","page":"Algorithms","text":"boundary = Vacuum() requests an isolated, non-periodic potential. The paper describes the four-step algorithm of James (1977), implemented in PhysicalFFT.jl: solve the Poisson equation with homogeneous Dirichlet boundaries, compute the induced surface charges, convolve them with the Green's function (in practice by direct summation over the boundary faces), and subtract the resulting boundary potential. That algorithm is mathcal O(N^4) for an N^3 grid. In the current WaveDM.jl driver, the same isolated-potential request is evaluated by mapping the grid density onto the mesh particles (with the mean subtracted in the kick-step solver) and calling the N-body tree solver in AstroNbodySim.jl with the configured SofteningLength; this avoids the mathcal O(N^4) surface sum but introduces a softening scale. Either route is intended for verification at modest resolution rather than for production runs.","title":"4.2 Vacuum (isolated)"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#4.3-Absorbing-layer","page":"Algorithms","text":"For simulations where wave energy must exit the domain without reflection, WaveDM.jl multiplies psi by a smooth absorbing mask mathcal B(mathbf r) at the end of every KDK step:\n\nmathcal B(mathbf r) = expleft-alphaleft(\n    6\n    - tanhfracx_i + L_x2L_xN_b\n    + tanhfracx_i - L_x2L_xN_b\n    - tanhfracy_j + L_y2L_yN_b\n    + tanhfracy_j - L_y2L_yN_b\n    - tanhfracz_k + L_z2L_zN_b\n    + tanhfracz_k - L_z2L_zN_b\nright)rightDelta t\n\nThe defaults are alpha = 10 (keyword absorb_coeff) and N_b = 50 (transition scale sim L50 sim mathcal O(10^2) cells). The mask limits unphysical interference patterns within the simulated halo.","title":"4.3 Absorbing layer"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#5.-Time-Integration","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"5. Time Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#5.1-Total-energy-drift","page":"Algorithms","text":"WaveDM.jl tracks three volume-integrated energies:\n\nKinetic — mathcal K = frac12int (rhomathbf v)^2  rhod^3 r, where the momentum density is rhomathbf v = (psi^*nablapsi - psinablapsi^*)(2i).\nGravitational potential — mathcal V = int rhoPhid^3 r.\nQuantum gradient — mathcal Q = int nablasqrtrho^2 d^3 r.\n\nThe total energy mathcal E = mathcal K + mathcal V + mathcal Q is approximately conserved for a virialized, isolated halo. In a fixed baryonic background with absorbing boundaries, however, mathcal E drifts as mass and energy leave the domain; with a time-dependent external potential, energy can also be exchanged with the host. This is a real physical effect, not a numerical artefact, and is monitored in real time.","title":"5.1 Total energy drift"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#5.2-Timestep-criterion","page":"Algorithms","text":"Following Schwabe et al. (2016), Mocz et al. (2017), May & Springel (2021) and May et al. (2023), WaveDM.jl uses\n\nDelta t le Delta t_max = 05minleft(\n    frac43pifracmhbarDelta x^2\n    2pifrachbarmfrac1maxPhi_c\n    2pifrachbarmfrac1kappamaxpsi^2\nright)\n\nwhere the three terms limit phase variations from the kinetic propagation, the gravitational potential, and the self-interaction respectively. In dimensionless units the prefactors mhbar are absorbed and the expression reads\n\nDelta t_max = 05minleft(\n    frac43piDelta x^2\n    frac2pimaxPhi_c\n    frac2pikappamaxpsi^2\nright)\n\nPass autoset_timestep = true to use it; the safety factor zeta_t is set by autoset_timestep_ratio (default 0.9).\n\nImplementation note. compute_timestep evaluates the three limits as 2Δx²/π, 2π/max|Φ|, and 2π/(|κ| max|ψ|), then multiplies the minimum by autoset_timestep_ratio. The first expression corresponds to half of 4Δx²/π; the paper's 2Δx²/(3π) is a more conservative choice. The self-interaction limit in the code uses the field amplitude max|ψ| rather than the density max|ψ|²; for a strong nonlinear term this makes the automatic step less restrictive than the paper formula.","title":"5.2 Timestep criterion"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#5.3-Simulation-duration","page":"Algorithms","text":"The ground-state oscillation period is tau_00 sim hbar(m_a v_rm vir^2) [Chiang et al. 2021]. For Crater II (v_rm virapprox 10 km s^-1, m_22=500) one obtains tau_00 approx 375 Myr with the hbar(m_a v_rm vir^2) convention used in the paper. Some works define tau_00=2pihbar(m_a v_rm vir^2), which is longer by a factor 2pi.\n\nThe relaxation time is set by the crossing time t_cross ≡ R_h/v_vir ≈ 0.74 Gyr (R_h/100 kpc)(130 km/s / v_vir). The Crater II runs in the paper start at t=-6 Gyr and reach virial equilibrium after approximately 4 Gyr of evolution (around tsim-2 Gyr). The production choice used there is Tmax = 6u\"Gyr\".","title":"5.3 Simulation duration"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#6.-Quasi-Stationary-Equilibrium-(QSS)-Diagnostics","page":"Algorithms","text":"Three nested criteria are provided, in order from global to spatially resolved. The same state is also referred to as a non-equilibrium quasi-stationary state (NEQSS); global virialization is a necessary but not sufficient condition for it.","title":"6. Quasi-Stationary Equilibrium (QSS) Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#6.1-Overall-energy-terms-(global,-cheap)","page":"Algorithms","text":"Monitor mathcal K(t), mathcal V(t), mathcal Q(t) and the virial term 2mathcal K+2mathcal Q+mathcal V. For the SPE, the second time derivative of the moment of inertia is\n\nddot I = mathcal V + 2mathcal K + 2mathcal Q\n\n[Hui et al. 2017]. After virialization, mathcal E approaches a constant and ddot I to 0, i.e. 2mathcal K+2mathcal Q+mathcal V approx 0. This is a necessary but not sufficient condition for a quasi-stationary state. The Poisson equation fixes Phi only up to a spatial constant; for the energy diagnostics the zero point is fixed using the final snapshot. This changes only the zero point, not the gravitational field or the dynamics.\n\nUse when: the analysis only needs integrated halo properties; you want the cheapest indicator.","title":"6.1 Overall energy terms (global, cheap)"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#6.2-Mass-fraction-radii-(spatial,-cheap)","page":"Algorithms","text":"Track the radii enclosing fixed mass fractions (10 %, 20 %, …, 90 %). These radii initially increase as mass redistributes outward during relaxation, then approach steady values. Stabilization of all nine curves indicates that the spatial configuration of rho(r) no longer evolves.\n\nUse when: the analysis requires that rho(r) be approximately stationary in time, e.g. when fitting a single density profile.","title":"6.2 Mass-fraction radii (spatial, cheap)"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#6.3-Velocity-dispersion-profile-(post-processing,-expensive)","page":"Algorithms","text":"For a halo in a quasi-stationary state supported by both velocity dispersion and rotational motion, the Jeans equation\n\nfracd(rhosigma_r^2)dr + frac2beta(rhosigma_r^2)r - rhofraclangle v_thetarangle^2 + langle v_phirangle^2r = -rhofracdPhi_rm totaldr - rhofracdQdr\n\nwhere beta = 1 - (sigma_theta^2 + sigma_phi^2)(2sigma_r^2) is the velocity-anisotropy parameter and Q is the quantum potential. The right-hand side represents the gravitational force and the quantum-pressure force; the three terms on the left correspond to the radial pressure-gradient term, the anisotropic-pressure correction, and centrifugal support from rotational streaming.\n\nAssuming isotropic velocity dispersion (beta equiv 0) and rearranging gives the predicted radial velocity dispersion\n\nsigma_r^2(r) = frac1rho(r) int_r^infty rho(r)left(fracdPhi_rm totaldr + fracdQdr - fraclangle v_thetarangle^2 + langle v_phirangle^2rright) dr\n\nFor an isolated halo this Jeans-derived sigma_r(r) matches the directly measured radial velocity dispersion, validating the QSS. With a time-dependent tidal field the steady-state assumption breaks down; the simulated and Jeans-predicted sigma_r no longer agree, signalling a tide-driven non-equilibrium state. The derivative dQdr involves second derivatives of the density and is sensitive to grid noise, so the density field is normally smoothed before this term is evaluated.\n\nUse when: the science goal is to compare with integral-field-spectroscopy observations; this is a post-processing diagnostic because it requires the velocity moments on the grid.","title":"6.3 Velocity-dispersion profile (post-processing, expensive)"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#7.-Initial-Conditions","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"7. Initial Conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#7.1-Density-profile","page":"Algorithms","text":"The macroscopic amplitude derives from a target density profile:\n\npsi = sqrtrho_rm halo  m\n\nwith the density profile chosen from gNFW, NFW, Zhao, or a user-supplied function:\n\ngNFW — rho(r) = rho_0   bigl(rr_s)^beta (1 + rr_s)^3-betabigr.\nNFW — beta = 1 special case of gNFW.\nZhao — rho(r) = rho_0(rr_s)^-gammabigl1 + (rr_s)^alphabigr^(gamma-beta)alpha, with inner slope gamma and outer slope beta.","title":"7.1 Density profile"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#7.2-Velocity-field","page":"Algorithms","text":"The phase theta(mathbf r) encodes the initial velocity field through mathbf v = nablatheta. WaveDM.jl uses direct integration of the velocity components by default. An alternative is to solve the Poisson equation nabla^2theta = nablacdotmathbf v with homogeneous Dirichlet boundaries. The reconstructed field is curl-free because it is the gradient of a scalar, not because of the Dirichlet condition; for a velocity field with non-zero curl, the reconstructed nablatheta is generally not equivalent to the original mathbf v.\n\nThe velocity magnitude is taken from the local circular velocity v_c = sqrtrdPhi_rm totaldr and assembled as a weighted sum of rotation and random motion:\n\nmathbf v = zeta_rm velbiglzeta_rm rotv_chatmathbf e_phi\n                                + (1-zeta_rm rot)v_chatmathbf e_rm randombigr\n\nwith zeta_rm vel controlled by velocity_ratio and zeta_rm rot by rotational_ratio.","title":"7.2 Velocity field"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#7.3-Pooling-strategy-(velocity-coarse-graining)","page":"Algorithms","text":"A fully random velocity field on each cell typically has too small a divergence, leading to underestimated phase gradients, repeated halo collapses, and unrealistic density distributions. To prevent this, WaveDM.jl implements a pooling strategy (bulk_perturb = true): within each bulk_size³ block of cells the velocity vectors are reset to the value of a reference cell — either the block's corner, the box centre, or a randomly placed cell. The current code default is bulk_size = 8; the Crater II convergence runs in the paper used bulk_size = 4. The pooling preserves velocity coherence without introducing spurious gradients.\n\nFor purely rotational velocity fields (zeta_rm rot=1) pooling is unnecessary; for mixed configurations it is a small but important correction.","title":"7.3 Pooling strategy (velocity coarse-graining)"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#7.4-Baryonic-initial-conditions","page":"Algorithms","text":"Baryons are placed either on the same mesh (baryon_mode = :mesh) or as N-body particles (baryon_mode = :particles_static / :particles_dynamic). The Milky Way mass model used in the case study follows Zhu et al. (2023):\n\nbeginaligned\nrho_rm bulge(r) = fracrho_0b(1+rr_0)^alphaexpbigl-(rr_rm cut)^2bigr \nrho_rm disk(R z) = fracSigma_02 z_dexpbigl(-zz_d - RR_dbigr) \nrho_rm gas(R z) = fracSigma_04 z_dexpbigl(-R_mR - RR_dbigr)rm sech^2(z2z_d) \nrho_rm DM(r) = rho_0hleft(fracrr_hright)^-gamma\n                 left1 + left(fracrr_hright)^alpharight^(gamma-beta)alpha\nendaligned\n\nwith r = sqrtR^2 + (zq)^2 in cylindrical coordinates. Sampling the baryons as N-body particles is illustrated in Examples.","title":"7.4 Baryonic initial conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#8.-Tidal-Field","page":"Algorithms","text":"When MW_tidal_field = true, the gravitational potential of the MW (and, optionally, the LMC with LMC_tidal_field = true) is added to Phi_rm total at every KDK step. The tidal field is evaluated at the satellite's instantaneous position mathbf r_rm sat(t) along its lookback orbit (Section 9), so the wave-CDM halo experiences a time-dependent external potential.\n\nTo prevent the tidal field from exerting a spurious net force on the halo, WaveDM.jl subtracts the mass-weighted mean (center-of-mass) acceleration\n\nmathbf a_rm cm(t)\n= fracint rho(mathbf rt)mathbf g_rm tidal(mathbf rt)d^3r\n       int rho(mathbf rt)d^3r\nqquad\nmathbf g_rm tidal = -nablaPhi_rm tidal\n\nfrom the tidal acceleration at every step. The potential is first referenced to its value at the density maximum, after which the gradient correction is applied. This removes the uniform translation component without changing the internal tidal field; without it the halo would acquire unphysical center-of-mass motion.","title":"8. Tidal Field"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#9.-Trajectory-Lookback","page":"Algorithms","text":"Trajectory lookback uses the positional and velocity data of MW satellites from Battaglia et al. (2022). The tool converts (RA, Dec, mu_alpha*, mu_delta, distance, radial velocity) to the Galactocentric frame and integrates the orbit backward in the (possibly time-dependent) MW potential.\n\nLMC perturbations are included by integrating the LMC orbit backward and adding its gravitational influence to the satellite's equation of motion. The LMC is modeled with M_rm LMC = 15times 10^11M_odot and r_s = 1084 kpc, and can be either an analytic Plummer potential or a system of N-body particles (particles_LMC).\n\nThe resulting trajectory mathbf r_rm sat(t) drives the time-dependent tidal field in Section 8. See the Examples page for a Crater II script.","title":"9. Trajectory Lookback"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#10.-Convergence-Test-Strategy","page":"Algorithms","text":"The following four axes are exercised in the validation suite (see Tests):\n\nTimestep Delta t — vary by factors of 0.5× and 2×. Should be negligible impact on density and velocity-dispersion profiles.\nSpatial resolution Delta x — vary by factors of 0.75× and 1.5×. Coarser grids lock the density into its initial distribution; finer grids improve interference-pattern resolution.\nDomain size L — vary by factors of 0.67× and 1.33×. Boundary artifacts become visible at small Lr_s.\nInitial condition parameters — zeta_rm rot (rotation), zeta_rm pool (bulk_size), and the absorber coefficient alpha.\n\nThe reference simulation uses m_22=50, N_x=384, L=40 kpc, N_t=377, zeta_rm rot=09, alpha=10, zeta_rm pool=4, and T_max=6 Gyr. Tests are run with the MW tidal field disabled so the halo is free to virialize cleanly. The paper also varies the absorber strength (alpha=01030100), the rotational fraction (zeta_rm rot=0030609), and the pooling size (zeta_rm pool=14816).","title":"10. Convergence Test Strategy"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#11.-Baryon-Gravity-and-Parallel-Execution","page":"Algorithms","text":"","title":"11. Baryon Gravity and Parallel Execution"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#11.1-Baryon-modes","page":"Algorithms","text":"Baryons are controlled by baryon_mode:\n\nMode Representation Typical use\n:ignored none DM-only halos\n:mesh same Cartesian grid as psi extended baryons (dSph, ETG, ICM)\n:particles_static N-body particles, fixed non-evolving host potential\n:particles_dynamic N-body particles, co-evolved fully coupled wave + baryon dynamics\n\nThe mesh mode reuses the same FFT Poisson solver as the wave dark matter. Resolving a stellar or gaseous disc on the mesh typically requires Delta x lesssim 005 kpc, so the mesh mode is best suited to spatially extended baryonic distributions. Particle modes avoid this resolution constraint and are the recommended choice for disc-dominated galaxies.\n\nParticle forces are computed with either DirectSum() (mathcal O(N^2)) or Tree() (mathcal O(Nlog N)) from AstroNbodySim.jl. Direct summation is suitable for Nlesssim10^5 and introduces no multipole truncation error; the tree is preferred at larger N. The tree solver follows the Gadget-2 framework: Peano–Hilbert keys define a global top-tree for work partitioning, an octree stores multipole data, and a cell is opened unless\n\nfracGMr^2left(fraclrright) le alphamathbf a\n\nwhere M and l are the node mass and side length, r is the distance from the particle to the node, and mathbf a is the particle's acceleration from the previous step. When alphato0 the tree degenerates to direct summation. Plummer softening prevents singular forces; the softening length is taken of order the mean interparticle spacing d=(VN)^13, with a default of 01 kpc in the current code.\n\nIn :particles_dynamic mode the coupling is bidirectional: particles contribute Phi_rm baryon to the grid every SPE step, and the wave-DM acceleration is interpolated back to the particle positions. The N-body integrator supports fixed and adaptive sub-steps, so multiple N-body steps are taken within one SPE step.","title":"11.1 Baryon modes"},{"category":"section","location":"algorithms/#11.2-Parallel-execution","page":"Algorithms","text":"WaveDM.jl combines three levels of parallelism:\n\nShared memory. Launch Julia with julia -t N; multi-threaded FFTW can be enabled with FFTW.set_provider!(\"mkl\") and FFTW.set_num_threads(Threads.nthreads()).\nDistributed memory. distributed_memory = true with Distributed.jl workers. In the current release the distributed FFT Poisson solver is not implemented (it raises an error); distributed workers are therefore useful for initial-condition generation and N-body work, but the full SSFM loop still runs on a single process. An MPI backend is planned.\nGPU. gpu = true dispatches FFTs and element-wise operations to CUDA.jl. GPU acceleration is most useful for Ngtrsim256^3; on a 12 GB device, Ngtrsim512 becomes memory-limited.\n\nThe same user script can switch between these backends through keyword arguments; no code changes are required.","title":"11.2 Parallel execution"}]
}
